insert into public.concepts (slug, topic, difficulty, pro, title, data) values ('power-apps-delegation', 'lowcode', 'beginner', false, 'Delegation in Power Apps', '{"slug":"power-apps-delegation","title":"Delegation in Power Apps","topic":"lowcode","difficulty":"beginner","minutes":6,"pro":false,"related":["dataverse","sql-indexes","supabase-rls"],"dek":"The blue underline that quietly turns \"show me all open orders\" into \"show me the open ones among the first 500\". What it means and how to design around it.","hook":"A Power Apps gallery works perfectly in testing with 200 rows. Six months later the list has 30,000 rows and users swear records are \"missing\". Nothing crashed. Nothing errored. The app has simply been filtering a small slice of the data the whole time — and the warning was there from day one.","yap":"Delegation is Power Apps asking the data source to do the filtering. When it can''t, it downloads the first 500 rows and filters those — no error, just quietly wrong results.","basics":"When you write a formula like:\n\n```powerfx\nFilter(Orders, Status = \"Open\")\n```\n\nPower Apps has two ways to run it:\n\n- **Delegate it**: translate the formula into a query the data source understands (SharePoint, Dataverse, SQL Server) and let the *server* return only the matching rows. Fast, and correct for any table size.\n- **Run it locally**: download rows to the device and filter them in the app. But Power Apps won''t download everything — only the first **500 rows** by default (you can raise it to **2,000** in settings).\n\nIf part of your formula can''t be translated for that data source, Power Apps falls back to local mode and shows a **delegation warning** (blue underline / yellow triangle).\n\nThe danger: with a 10,000-row list, a non-delegable filter only looks at the first 500 rows. Records 501–10,000 are invisible. No error message.\n\nThe fix is to rewrite the formula using functions and operators that **are** delegable for your data source.","hood":"### What''s delegable depends on the source\n\nEach connector declares what it can translate. Broadly:\n\n- **Dataverse** and **SQL Server** support the widest range: `Filter`, `Sort`, `Search` (Dataverse), `LookUp`, comparison operators, `StartsWith`, `And/Or/Not`, many aggregate functions.\n- **SharePoint** supports fewer: `Filter` and `LookUp` with `=`, `<`, `>`, `StartsWith` on text, and some column types only. `Search` and `in` (substring) are not delegable.\n- **Excel** (as a data source): effectively not delegable at all.\n\nAlways check the connector''s delegation documentation, not a general list.\n\n### Common delegation breakers\n\n```powerfx\n// ❌ Not delegable to SharePoint: ''in'' substring search\nFilter(Tickets, txtSearch.Text in Title)\n\n// ✅ Delegable: prefix search\nFilter(Tickets, StartsWith(Title, txtSearch.Text))\n```\n\nOther usual suspects: `Len`, `Left/Right/Mid`, `Upper/Lower` on columns, `CountRows` on large sources, filtering on calculated or lookup columns, and functions applied to the column side of a comparison.\n\n### The row limit setting\n\n**Settings → General → Data row limit** goes from 500 up to 2,000. Raising it hides the problem a little longer; it doesn''t solve it. Setting it to 1 temporarily is a great trick to *test* which screens depend on non-delegable formulas.","deep":"### Designing for scale from day one\n\n1. **Choose the data source deliberately.** For anything that will grow beyond a few thousand rows or needs relationships, Dataverse or SQL beats SharePoint lists.\n2. **Filter first, decorate later.** Apply delegable filters to shrink the server result, then run non-delegable logic on that smaller set: `AddColumns(Filter(Orders, Region = varRegion), \"Label\", …)`.\n3. **Index the columns you filter on.** In SharePoint, lists beyond the 5,000-item list view threshold need indexed columns for filtered queries to work at all.\n4. **Push heavy logic server-side.** Dataverse views, SQL views or stored procedures, or Power Automate flows can return pre-filtered data.\n\n### Collections are not a fix\n\n`ClearCollect(colAll, Orders)` is subject to the same row limit. Loops that page through data with `ForAll` work but are slow, fragile and hammer the connector. If you need all rows locally, the design is usually wrong.\n\n### Explaining it to stakeholders\n\nDelegation is a *data architecture* conversation, not a formula trick. The question to ask early: \"How many rows will this table have in two years, and how will users search it?\" That answer decides between SharePoint and Dataverse, which decides licensing — which is often why projects start on SharePoint and hit the wall later.\n\n### The SQL mental model\n\nDelegation is essentially \"can this formula become a `WHERE` clause?\". If a SQL database could answer it with a query, it''s usually delegable to SQL Server. If you''d need to pull every row into application memory to evaluate it, it isn''t.","myths":[{"myth":"Delegation warnings are cosmetic","truth":"They mean your results may be silently incomplete once the data grows past the row limit."},{"myth":"Raising the row limit to 2,000 solves it","truth":"It postpones the problem. Any source that grows past 2,000 rows breaks the same way."},{"myth":"Collecting data into a collection avoids delegation","truth":"`Collect` and `ClearCollect` are bound by the same row limit."}],"facts":["The default data row limit is 500 and the maximum is 2,000.","Setting the row limit to 1 is a widely used debugging trick for finding non-delegable formulas."],"realWorld":["Search boxes over large SharePoint lists rewritten from `in` to `StartsWith`.","Migrating growing apps from SharePoint lists to Dataverse for delegation and security roles.","Power Platform consultants auditing apps for delegation warnings before go-live."],"check":{"prompt":"A SharePoint list has 12,000 rows and the data row limit is 2,000. A gallery uses a non-delegable filter. What do users see?","options":["An error message telling them to narrow the search","Matches found only among the first 2,000 rows, with no error","All matching rows, just more slowly","An empty gallery"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Non-delegable formulas run locally on at most the data row limit. Matches beyond row 2,000 are silently missing."}}'::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug) do update set topic = excluded.topic, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, pro = excluded.pro, title = excluded.title, data = excluded.data, updated_at = now();
insert into public.concepts (slug, topic, difficulty, pro, title, data) values ('python-gil', 'languages', 'advanced', false, 'Python''s GIL, and why it''s finally optional', '{"slug":"python-gil","title":"Python''s GIL, and why it''s finally optional","topic":"languages","difficulty":"advanced","minutes":6,"pro":false,"related":["nodejs-event-loop","js-event-loop","llm-tokens"],"dek":"For thirty years one lock decided that Python threads take turns. Here''s what it protects, when it hurts, and what changed in 3.13.","hook":"Ask why Python threads don''t speed up number-crunching and someone will say \"the GIL\" like it''s a curse. It''s actually a pragmatic engineering trade-off that made CPython simple, fast for single-threaded code, and easy to extend in C. The interesting question isn''t \"why does it exist?\" but \"why was it so hard to remove?\"","yap":"The GIL doesn''t stop Python from doing many things at once — it stops it from *computing* many things at once. For I/O, threads are fine; for CPU, use processes or the new free-threaded build.","basics":"The **Global Interpreter Lock** is a lock inside CPython (the standard Python interpreter). Only the thread holding it can run Python code. Threads take turns.\n\nWhat that means in practice:\n\n- **I/O-bound work** (web requests, database calls, reading files): threads work well. While one thread waits for the network, it releases the GIL and another runs.\n- **CPU-bound work** (parsing, maths, image processing in pure Python): threads don''t help. Eight threads still execute one at a time.\n\n```python\nfrom concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor, ProcessPoolExecutor\n\n# Good: downloading 50 URLs — mostly waiting\nwith ThreadPoolExecutor(10) as ex:\n    pages = list(ex.map(fetch, urls))\n\n# Good: CPU-heavy work — separate processes, separate GILs\nwith ProcessPoolExecutor() as ex:\n    results = list(ex.map(crunch, chunks))\n```\n\nProcesses each have their own interpreter and their own GIL, so they truly run in parallel — at the cost of more memory and data copying between them.","hood":"### What the GIL actually protects\n\nCPython manages memory with **reference counting**: every object tracks how many references point to it, and is freed when the count hits zero. Incrementing and decrementing those counts from multiple threads without coordination would corrupt memory. One big lock is the simplest way to make every refcount change safe — and it''s cheaper than locking each object individually.\n\nIt also made C extensions easy: extension authors could assume no other Python code runs while they hold the GIL.\n\n### When it''s released\n\n- During blocking I/O (sockets, files, `time.sleep`).\n- Periodically: the interpreter asks the running thread to drop the GIL every 5ms by default (`sys.getswitchinterval()`), so other threads get a turn.\n- By C extensions doing long computations. NumPy releases it inside many array operations, which is why NumPy-heavy code *can* benefit from threads.\n\n### It doesn''t make your code thread-safe\n\nThe GIL protects the interpreter''s internals, not your logic. `counter += 1` is several bytecode operations; a thread switch can happen between them. You still need `threading.Lock` for shared mutable state.","deep":"### PEP 703 and free-threaded Python\n\nPython 3.13 (October 2024) shipped an experimental **free-threaded build** (`python3.13t`) with the GIL removed, the result of PEP 703 by Sam Gross. In 3.14 the free-threaded build became officially supported (no longer experimental), though the default build still has the GIL. Making it work required:\n\n- **Biased reference counting**: the owning thread updates refcounts cheaply; other threads use atomic operations.\n- **Immortal objects** (PEP 683): objects like `None` and small ints never get refcounted, removing contention on the hottest objects.\n- **Per-object locks** for built-in containers like `dict` and `list`.\n- A new memory allocator (mimalloc) that''s thread-safe.\n\nThe cost: single-threaded code runs somewhat slower in the free-threaded build, and C extensions must be rebuilt and audited for thread safety. The ecosystem is migrating gradually.\n\n### Other routes around the GIL\n\n- **Subinterpreters** (PEP 734, `concurrent.interpreters` in 3.14): multiple interpreters in one process, each with its own GIL.\n- **asyncio**: one thread, cooperative concurrency — excellent for many concurrent I/O operations, no help for CPU.\n- **Native code**: Rust (via PyO3), Cython, or Numba releasing the GIL for hot loops.\n\n### Why removal took 30 years\n\nEarlier attempts (Greg Stein''s 1999 patch, Larry Hastings'' \"Gilectomy\") made single-threaded Python significantly slower. Guido van Rossum''s long-standing condition was that removing the GIL must not slow down single-threaded code much. PEP 703''s techniques finally got the penalty low enough to be acceptable.","myths":[{"myth":"Python threads are fake","truth":"They''re real OS threads. They just can''t execute Python bytecode simultaneously in the default build."},{"myth":"The GIL makes my code thread-safe","truth":"It protects interpreter internals. Your read-modify-write operations can still race."},{"myth":"asyncio removes the GIL problem","truth":"asyncio is single-threaded concurrency. It''s great for I/O but doesn''t parallelise CPU work."}],"facts":["PyPy and CPython both have a GIL; Jython and IronPython never did.","Ruby''s main interpreter has an equivalent lock, called the GVL."],"realWorld":["Web servers like Gunicorn run multiple worker *processes* precisely to use every core.","Data pipelines use `multiprocessing` or Dask/Ray for CPU-heavy transforms.","ML training loops spend their time in C/CUDA code that releases the GIL, which is why Python dominates ML despite it."],"check":{"prompt":"A Python script resizes 1,000 images using a pure-Python library. Which change is most likely to make it use all 8 cores (default CPython build)?","options":["Use `threading` with 8 threads","Use `ProcessPoolExecutor`","Rewrite it with `asyncio`","Increase `sys.setswitchinterval`"],"answer":1,"explanation":"CPU-bound pure-Python work needs separate interpreters to run in parallel. Processes each have their own GIL. Threads and asyncio would still execute the resizing one step at a time."}}'::jsonb)
