insert into public.concepts (slug, topic, difficulty, pro, title, data) values ('sql-indexes', 'databases', 'beginner', false, 'Database indexes are sorted shortcuts', '{"slug":"sql-indexes","title":"Database indexes are sorted shortcuts","topic":"databases","difficulty":"beginner","minutes":6,"pro":false,"related":["postgres-mvcc","caching-strategies","supabase-rls"],"dek":"Why one line of SQL can turn a 4-second query into a 4-millisecond one — and why adding indexes everywhere makes things worse.","hook":"Every slow-app war story eventually reaches the same plot twist: \"there was no index.\" Indexes are the cheapest big performance win in software. They''re also widely misunderstood — people add them to every column, then wonder why writes got slow and the query planner ignores half of them.","yap":"An index is a sorted copy of a column with pointers back to the rows. It turns \"read everything\" into \"jump to the right page\" — and every write pays to keep that copy sorted.","basics":"Without an index, finding rows is like finding a word in a book with no index at the back: you read every page. Databases call this a **sequential scan**.\n\n```sql\nSELECT * FROM users WHERE email = ''asha@example.com'';\n```\n\nOn a million-row table, that''s a million rows checked to find one.\n\nAn **index** is a separate, sorted structure built from one or more columns, with each entry pointing to the matching row. With an index on `email`, the database jumps almost directly to the right entry.\n\n```sql\nCREATE INDEX users_email_idx ON users (email);\n```\n\nWhen indexes help:\n\n- Columns you filter on often (`WHERE`)\n- Columns you join on (`JOIN … ON orders.user_id = users.id`)\n- Columns you sort by (`ORDER BY created_at`)\n\nThe cost: every `INSERT`, `UPDATE` and `DELETE` must update every index on the table. Indexes also use disk and memory. They''re a trade: faster reads, slower writes.","hood":"### B-trees: the default\n\nMost indexes are **B-trees**: balanced trees where each node holds many sorted keys. A million rows typically fit in a tree only 3–4 levels deep, so a lookup touches just a handful of pages. Because keys are sorted, B-trees support `=`, `<`, `>`, `BETWEEN`, prefix `LIKE ''abc%''` and `ORDER BY` — but not `LIKE ''%abc''`.\n\n### Composite indexes and column order\n\n```sql\nCREATE INDEX ON orders (customer_id, created_at);\n```\n\nThis is sorted by `customer_id` first, then `created_at` within each customer — like a phone book sorted by last name, then first name. It helps queries filtering on `customer_id`, or on `customer_id` *and* `created_at`. It doesn''t help a query filtering only by `created_at`. Put equality-filtered columns first, range/sort columns after.\n\n### The planner decides\n\nThe database''s **query planner** estimates costs using table statistics. If your query matches 40% of the table, reading the index then jumping to each row is slower than a straight scan — so it won''t use the index. That''s correct behaviour, not a bug. Check with:\n\n```sql\nEXPLAIN ANALYZE SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = 42;\n```\n\nLook for `Index Scan` vs `Seq Scan`, and compare estimated vs actual rows.\n\n### Functions break indexes\n\n`WHERE lower(email) = ''a@b.com''` can''t use an index on `email`. Create an **expression index**: `CREATE INDEX ON users (lower(email))`.","deep":"### Covering indexes and index-only scans\n\nIf the index contains every column a query needs, the database can answer from the index alone. In Postgres, `INCLUDE` adds non-key columns:\n\n```sql\nCREATE INDEX ON orders (customer_id, created_at DESC) INCLUDE (total);\n```\n\nPostgres must still check visibility (see MVCC); the **visibility map** lets it skip the table for pages known to be all-visible, which is why freshly vacuumed tables get faster index-only scans.\n\n### Other index types in Postgres\n\n- **Hash**: equality only; rarely better than B-tree.\n- **GIN**: inverted index for \"contains\" queries — JSONB keys, arrays, full-text search, trigram search with `pg_trgm`.\n- **GiST / SP-GiST**: geometric data, ranges, nearest-neighbour.\n- **BRIN**: tiny indexes for huge, naturally ordered tables (time-series logs) that store min/max per block range.\n- **HNSW / IVFFlat** (via pgvector): approximate nearest-neighbour search over embeddings.\n\n### Partial indexes\n\n```sql\nCREATE INDEX ON tasks (due_at) WHERE completed = false;\n```\n\nIndex only the rows you query. Smaller, faster, cheaper to maintain.\n\n### Write amplification and the real cost\n\nEach index adds write work, WAL volume and memory pressure. In Postgres, an update that changes an indexed column can''t use a **HOT update** (heap-only tuple), so it touches every index. Unused indexes are pure cost — `pg_stat_user_indexes.idx_scan = 0` finds them.\n\n### Selectivity\n\nAn index on a boolean column with 50/50 distribution is nearly useless; one on a column where each value matches 0.01% of rows is gold. Cardinality matters more than \"is it in the WHERE clause?\".","myths":[{"myth":"More indexes always mean a faster database","truth":"Each index slows down writes and uses memory. Unused ones are pure overhead."},{"myth":"If an index exists, the database will use it","truth":"The planner skips indexes when a scan is cheaper — e.g. when the query returns a large fraction of the table."},{"myth":"Column order in a composite index doesn''t matter","truth":"It matters a lot. `(a, b)` helps queries on `a` or `a+b`, not queries on `b` alone."}],"facts":["The B-tree was introduced by Rudolf Bayer and Edward McCreight in 1970 at Boeing; what the \"B\" stands for has never been settled.","Postgres automatically creates an index for every `PRIMARY KEY` and `UNIQUE` constraint — but not for foreign keys."],"realWorld":["Adding an index on a foreign key (`orders.user_id`) to fix slow joins and slow cascading deletes.","Supabase''s Performance Advisor flags unindexed foreign keys and unused indexes.","`pg_trgm` GIN indexes powering fuzzy search boxes without a separate search engine."],"check":{"prompt":"A table has `CREATE INDEX ON events (user_id, created_at)`. Which query benefits *least*?","options":["`WHERE user_id = 7`","`WHERE user_id = 7 AND created_at > now() - interval ''1 day''`","`WHERE created_at > now() - interval ''1 day''`","`WHERE user_id = 7 ORDER BY created_at DESC LIMIT 10`"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The index is sorted by `user_id` first. Without a `user_id` condition, the matching `created_at` values are scattered across every user''s section of the index, so it can''t seek efficiently."}}'::jsonb)
