insert into public.concepts (slug, topic, difficulty, pro, title, data) values ('supabase-rls', 'backend', 'intermediate', false, 'Row Level Security, or why your anon key can be public', '{"slug":"supabase-rls","title":"Row Level Security, or why your anon key can be public","topic":"backend","difficulty":"intermediate","minutes":6,"pro":false,"related":["jwt-auth","postgres-mvcc","sql-indexes"],"dek":"Supabase lets the browser talk straight to Postgres. That''s only safe because the database itself decides which rows each user may see.","hook":"The first time you see a Supabase app, something feels wrong: the frontend queries the database directly, with a key sitting in the JavaScript bundle for anyone to copy. No API layer. No backend checks. The reason this isn''t a security disaster is a Postgres feature that''s been around since 2016 — and the reason some Supabase apps *are* disasters is forgetting to turn it on.","yap":"In Supabase, the API key just says which project you''re talking to. Who you are comes from the JWT, and what you can see is decided by RLS policies inside Postgres.","basics":"Normally an app looks like this: browser → your API server → database. Your API checks \"is this user allowed?\" before running queries.\n\nSupabase removes the middle layer. The browser talks to an auto-generated REST API (PostgREST) that runs queries directly against Postgres. So where do the permission checks go? **Into the database**, as **Row Level Security (RLS)** policies.\n\n```sql\nalter table notes enable row level security;\n\ncreate policy \"Users read their own notes\"\non notes for select\nusing (auth.uid() = user_id);\n```\n\nNow, when anyone runs `select * from notes`, Postgres silently adds the policy as a filter. A user only ever gets their own notes — even if they edit the frontend code, call the API with curl, or try to guess IDs.\n\nTwo keys matter:\n\n- **Anon / publishable key**: public, goes in the browser. It has only the permissions your RLS policies grant.\n- **Service-role / secret key**: bypasses RLS completely. Server-only. Never in the frontend, never in Git.","hood":"### How Postgres knows who you are\n\nWhen a user logs in, Supabase Auth issues a **JWT** containing their user ID (`sub`) and role (`authenticated`). Every API request carries it. PostgREST verifies the signature, switches to the matching Postgres role, and exposes the claims to SQL. `auth.uid()` simply reads the `sub` claim.\n\n### USING vs WITH CHECK\n\n- `USING (…)` filters which **existing rows** a query can see or affect (SELECT, UPDATE, DELETE).\n- `WITH CHECK (…)` validates **new or changed rows** (INSERT, UPDATE).\n\n```sql\ncreate policy \"insert own\" on notes for insert\nto authenticated\nwith check (auth.uid() = user_id);\n```\n\nWithout `WITH CHECK`, a user could insert notes with someone else''s `user_id`.\n\n### Default deny\n\nWith RLS enabled and **no policies**, nobody (except the service role and table owner) can read or write anything. That''s the safe default. The dangerous state is RLS *disabled* on a table exposed through the API — then the anon key can read everything.\n\n### Policies per command and role\n\nPolicies are permissive by default and combined with OR. Scope them: `for select to authenticated` is clearer and faster than one catch-all policy.","deep":"### Performance: policies run per row\n\nA policy is effectively an extra `WHERE` clause. Two habits keep it fast:\n\n```sql\n-- Wrap auth functions in a subquery so Postgres evaluates them once per query\nusing ((select auth.uid()) = user_id)\n```\n\nand **index the columns your policies filter on** (`user_id`). Supabase''s own benchmarks show order-of-magnitude differences on large tables from these two changes alone.\n\n### Team and org access\n\nMembership checks often look like:\n\n```sql\nusing (exists (\n  select 1 from team_members m\n  where m.team_id = projects.team_id and m.user_id = (select auth.uid())\n))\n```\n\nOn hot paths, wrap the membership lookup in a `security definer` function returning the user''s team IDs, so it isn''t re-evaluated for every row (and isn''t itself subject to RLS recursion).\n\n### Things RLS doesn''t do\n\n- **Column-level control**: RLS is about rows. Hide columns with views, column privileges, or separate tables.\n- **Rate limiting or business workflows**: \"a user may start one free trial\" belongs in a `security definer` function or an Edge Function, not a policy that users could race.\n- **Protect against the service role**: anything holding that key bypasses RLS by design.\n\n### Testing policies\n\nImpersonate a user in SQL to verify behaviour:\n\n```sql\nset local role authenticated;\nset local request.jwt.claims = ''{\"sub\":\"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001\"}'';\nselect * from notes; -- should return only that user''s rows\n```\n\nTools like pgTAP let you keep these as automated tests in your migrations.","myths":[{"myth":"The anon key is a secret","truth":"It''s designed to be public. Security comes from RLS, not from hiding the key."},{"myth":"Filtering by user_id in the frontend is enough","truth":"Anyone can call the API directly. Only database-enforced policies count."},{"myth":"RLS makes queries slow","truth":"Well-written policies with indexed columns add negligible overhead."}],"facts":["Row Level Security arrived in PostgreSQL 9.5 (2016), long before Supabase existed.","Supabase''s dashboard and security advisor warn about tables exposed to the API with RLS disabled — the most common Supabase security mistake."],"realWorld":["Multi-tenant SaaS: every row has a `tenant_id`, and policies isolate customers.","This app: your XP, answers and saved concepts are protected by policies like `auth.uid() = user_id`.","Realtime subscriptions in Supabase also respect RLS, so users only receive changes to rows they can see."],"check":{"prompt":"A table has RLS enabled and this single policy: `create policy p on posts for select using (true)`. What can an authenticated user do?","options":["Read, insert, update and delete all posts","Read all posts, but not insert, update or delete","Nothing — `true` is not a valid policy","Only read their own posts"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The policy only covers `select`, and `true` lets every row through. With no insert/update/delete policies, those operations are denied by default."}}'::jsonb)
