insert into public.concepts (slug, topic, difficulty, pro, title, data) values ('typescript-structural-typing', 'languages', 'intermediate', false, 'TypeScript checks shapes, not names', '{"slug":"typescript-structural-typing","title":"TypeScript checks shapes, not names","topic":"languages","difficulty":"intermediate","minutes":5,"pro":false,"related":["js-event-loop","rest-idempotency"],"dek":"If it has the right properties, it fits. Structural typing is why TypeScript feels flexible — and why a UserId can quietly become an OrderId.","hook":"In Java or C#, a `Dog` is only a `Dog` if you declared it so. TypeScript doesn''t care what you called something; it cares what it *has*. This one design decision explains half of TypeScript''s ergonomics and most of its surprises.","yap":"TypeScript doesn''t ask \"what is this?\", it asks \"what can this do?\". Two types with the same shape are the same type — whatever you named them.","basics":"TypeScript compares types by their **structure** — their properties and methods — not by their names.\n\n```ts\ninterface Point { x: number; y: number }\n\nfunction print(p: Point) { console.log(p.x, p.y) }\n\nconst location = { x: 10, y: 20, label: ''office'' }\nprint(location) // ✅ fine — it has x and y\n```\n\n`location` was never declared as a `Point`. It doesn''t matter. It has `x: number` and `y: number`, so it fits. Extra properties are allowed when you pass a variable.\n\nThis matches how JavaScript is actually written: objects are created ad hoc, passed around, and used for what they contain. It''s sometimes called \"duck typing, checked at compile time\": if it walks like a duck…\n\nThe flip side:\n\n```ts\ntype Email = string\ntype Password = string\nfunction login(email: Email, password: Password) {}\nlogin(password, email) // ✅ compiles. Oops.\n```\n\nBoth are just `string`. The names are aliases, not new types.","hood":"### Excess property checks: the one exception\n\n```ts\nprint({ x: 1, y: 2, label: ''x'' })\n// ❌ Object literal may only specify known properties\n```\n\nFresh object literals get an extra check, because a property that doesn''t exist on the target is probably a typo (`colour` vs `color`). Assign it to a variable first and the check disappears — the object is no longer \"fresh\".\n\n### Assignability goes one way\n\nA type with *more* properties is assignable to one with *fewer* (it''s a subtype). Not the other way round. `{ x, y, z }` fits where `{ x, y }` is expected; `{ x }` doesn''t.\n\n### Functions are compared structurally too\n\n```ts\ntype Handler = (e: MouseEvent) => void\nconst log: Handler = () => console.log(''clicked'') // ✅ fewer params is fine\n```\n\nIgnoring parameters is always safe, so a function that takes fewer arguments is assignable. That''s why `array.forEach(x => …)` works even though `forEach` passes three arguments.\n\n### Classes are structural as well\n\nTwo classes with identical public members are interchangeable to the type checker — unless they have `private` or `#private` members, which make the class effectively nominal.","deep":"### Branded types: opting into nominal typing\n\nWhen mixing up IDs would be a real bug, add a phantom property that no plain string has:\n\n```ts\ntype UserId = string & { readonly __brand: ''UserId'' }\ntype OrderId = string & { readonly __brand: ''OrderId'' }\n\nconst UserId = (s: string) => s as UserId\n\nfunction cancel(id: OrderId) {}\ncancel(UserId(''u_1'')) // ❌ Type ''UserId'' is not assignable to ''OrderId''\n```\n\nThere''s no runtime cost — the brand exists only in the type system. Libraries like zod and Effect offer brand helpers; validation at the boundary is the natural place to mint branded values.\n\n### Variance and the method bivariance hole\n\nTypeScript checks function parameters contravariantly under `strictFunctionTypes` — but **methods** declared with method syntax are still checked bivariantly for compatibility with existing JS patterns. That''s a deliberate soundness gap. TypeScript''s design goals explicitly do not include a provably sound type system; usefulness for real JavaScript wins.\n\n### Why structural?\n\nTypeScript had to describe existing JavaScript, where objects are open and created everywhere without classes. A nominal system would have forced declarations everywhere and broken interop with untyped code. Go chose structural interfaces for a similar reason: decoupling. A type satisfies an interface without importing it.\n\n### Practical consequences\n\n- Mocks in tests are easy: any object with the right shape works.\n- API response types must be validated at runtime — the compiler trusts your `as User`, it doesn''t check the JSON.\n- Refactors that rename types don''t break anything; refactors that change shapes do.","myths":[{"myth":"Declaring `type UserId = string` makes a new type","truth":"It''s an alias. It''s interchangeable with every other string."},{"myth":"TypeScript checks your API responses","truth":"Types are erased at runtime. `res.json() as User` is a promise you make, not a check TypeScript does."},{"myth":"Extra properties are always errors","truth":"Only on fresh object literals. Variables with extra properties are fine."}],"facts":["TypeScript''s official design goals list \"apply a sound or provably correct type system\" as a **non-goal**.","Go, OCaml''s object system and Elm records are also structurally typed; Java, C# and Rust are nominal."],"realWorld":["Branded IDs in large codebases to stop cross-entity ID bugs.","Validating external data with zod/valibot and inferring types from the schema.","Writing lightweight test doubles without mocking frameworks."],"check":{"prompt":"Does this compile?","code":{"lang":"ts","source":"interface Named { name: string }\nconst p = { name: ''Asha'', age: 31 }\nconst n: Named = p"},"options":["Yes — `p` has a `name: string`, and extra properties are fine for a non-literal","No — `age` is an excess property","No — `p` was never declared as `Named`","Only with `strict: false`"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Structural typing: `p`''s shape includes everything `Named` needs. Excess property checks only apply to object literals written directly at the assignment."}}'::jsonb)
