insert into public.topics (slug, category, title, data) values ('cloud-computing', 'data-cloud', 'Cloud computing', '{"slug":"cloud-computing","title":"Cloud computing","category":"data-cloud","aliases":["cloud","AWS","Azure","GCP","IaaS","PaaS","SaaS","serverless","Lambda"],"tagline":"Renting computing, storage and services over the internet instead of buying and running your own servers.","level":"beginner","minutes":4,"concepts":["caching-strategies"],"related":["docker","kubernetes","system-design"],"popular":true,"what":"Cloud computing means using computing resources, like servers, storage, databases and AI models, that a provider runs for you in their data centres, and paying for what you use. AWS, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud are the biggest providers.","why":"Running your own servers means buying hardware up front, guessing how much you''ll need, and paying people to keep it running. If you guess low, the site goes down under traffic. If you guess high, you''ve wasted money. The cloud turns that into an on-demand bill: scale up in minutes, scale down when it''s quiet.","how":"Providers run huge fleets of servers and use **virtualisation** to split them into isolated pieces they rent out. You use them at different levels of abstraction:\n- **IaaS** (Infrastructure as a Service): virtual machines and networks you manage. Example: AWS EC2.\n- **PaaS** (Platform as a Service): you deploy code; they run the servers. Example: Azure App Service, Vercel.\n- **SaaS** (Software as a Service): finished applications. Example: Gmail, Microsoft 365.\n- **Serverless:** you upload functions that run on demand, billed per call. Example: AWS Lambda.","where":["Hosting websites, APIs and mobile back ends","Storing files and backups (Amazon S3, Azure Blob Storage)","Managed databases, so nobody patches database servers at 2 a.m.","Running AI models and data pipelines"],"know":["**Shared responsibility:** the provider secures the infrastructure; you secure your configuration, data and access.","**Regions and availability zones:** data centres in different places, for latency and resilience.","**Elasticity:** scale resources up and down with demand.","**Pay-as-you-go** can be cheaper or much more expensive. Cost monitoring is a real job.","**Managed services** trade control for less operations work."],"analogy":"The cloud is like electricity from the grid instead of running your own generator. You plug in, use as much as you need, and pay the bill. You still have to avoid leaving everything switched on.","talk10":"Cloud computing means renting servers, storage and services from providers like AWS or Azure, and paying for what you use instead of running your own hardware.","talk30":"With cloud computing, instead of buying and maintaining servers, you rent resources from providers like AWS, Azure or Google Cloud and scale them up or down on demand. You can work at different levels: raw virtual machines, platforms where you just deploy code, or serverless functions that run per request. The big benefits are speed and elasticity. The things to watch are costs, which can grow quietly, and security, because you''re still responsible for configuring access correctly.","comebacks":[{"q":"Is the cloud always cheaper?","a":"Not always. For steady, predictable workloads, owning hardware can be cheaper. The cloud wins on flexibility and speed."},{"q":"What''s serverless?","a":"You still use servers; you just don''t manage them. You deploy functions and pay per execution."},{"q":"Is the cloud secure?","a":"The infrastructure is usually very secure. Most breaches come from customer misconfiguration, like a public storage bucket."}],"facts":["AWS launched S3 and EC2 in 2006, which is generally considered the start of modern public cloud.","Many well-known data leaks came from storage buckets accidentally left public."],"deeper":"### Choosing a level of abstraction\n- Full control of the OS → virtual machines (IaaS)\n- Just run my web app → PaaS (App Service, Vercel, Render)\n- Spiky, event-driven work → serverless functions\n- Many services, one platform → containers on Kubernetes or a managed container service\n\n### Cost traps\n- Data **egress** (data leaving the cloud) is often billed.\n- Idle resources keep billing.\n- Serverless is cheap at low volume and can get expensive at very high steady volume.","sources":[{"title":"NIST definition of cloud computing (SP 800-145)","url":"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/145/final"},{"title":"AWS: Shared responsibility model","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/compliance/shared-responsibility-model/"}],"check":{"prompt":"A company''s customer files leak because a storage bucket was set to public. Under the shared responsibility model, who was responsible?","options":["The cloud provider","The customer, because access configuration is their responsibility","Nobody, it''s a platform bug","The internet service provider"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Providers secure the physical infrastructure and the service itself. Configuring who can access your data is the customer''s job."},"interview":[{"id":"cloud-computing-iq1","q":"Explain IaaS, PaaS and SaaS.","level":"beginner","pro":false,"answer":"1. IaaS: rent virtual machines, storage and networks; you manage the OS and above.\n2. PaaS: deploy your code; the provider manages servers and runtime.\n3. SaaS: use a finished application.\n4. Moving up the list means less control and less operations work.","short":"They''re levels of how much the provider manages. With IaaS you get virtual machines and manage the rest. With PaaS you just deploy code. With SaaS you just use the software, like Gmail.","detail":"A pizza analogy is common: make it at home, take-and-bake, delivery, eat out. Better is a real example from your own work: \"Our API runs on Azure App Service, which is PaaS, so we don''t patch servers.\"","followups":[{"q":"Where does serverless fit?","a":"Usually counted as a form of PaaS, or \"functions as a service\", billed per execution."}],"mistakes":["Not mentioning the control vs management trade-off"]},{"id":"cloud-computing-iq2","q":"How would you make an application highly available in the cloud?","level":"intermediate","pro":true,"answer":"1. Run multiple instances across availability zones behind a load balancer.\n2. Use managed databases with replicas and automatic failover.\n3. Make the app stateless so any instance can serve any request.\n4. Health checks and auto-scaling.\n5. Backups and a tested recovery plan.","short":"I''d run several stateless instances across availability zones behind a load balancer, use a managed database with a standby replica, and add health checks and auto-scaling. Then I''d test that failover actually works.","detail":"Mention the difference between high availability (survive a failure with little downtime) and disaster recovery (recover from losing a whole region). Mention RTO and RPO if the conversation goes deeper.","followups":[{"q":"What are RTO and RPO?","a":"Recovery time objective: how quickly you must be back. Recovery point objective: how much data loss is acceptable."}],"mistakes":["Storing sessions in memory on a single instance"]}]}'::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug) do update set category = excluded.category, title = excluded.title, data = excluded.data, updated_at = now();
insert into public.topics (slug, category, title, data) values ('dataverse', 'enterprise', 'Dataverse', '{"slug":"dataverse","title":"Dataverse","category":"enterprise","aliases":["Microsoft Dataverse","Common Data Service","CDS","Dynamics 365 data","tables","security roles","Power Platform"],"tagline":"The managed database behind the Power Platform, with business rules and security built in.","level":"intermediate","minutes":4,"concepts":["dataverse","power-apps-delegation"],"related":["power-apps","power-automate","postgresql"],"popular":false,"what":"Microsoft Dataverse is the data platform underneath Power Apps, Power Automate and Dynamics 365. It stores data in **tables** with typed **columns** and **relationships**, like a relational database, and adds business logic, security roles, auditing and APIs on top, without you managing a server.","why":"SharePoint lists and Excel files are fine for small, simple data. They struggle with relationships, large volumes, fine-grained security and business rules. A full SQL database needs developers and infrastructure. Dataverse is in between: real relational data with enterprise security, managed by Microsoft and usable from low-code tools.","how":"You define tables (standard ones like Account and Contact, or your own custom tables), columns with types, and relationships (one-to-many, many-to-many). Security is **role-based**: security roles grant create, read, write and delete rights at levels like user, business unit or organisation. Business rules, calculated columns, plug-ins and flows add logic. Everything is available through a **Web API** (OData REST).","where":["Data layer for model-driven and canvas Power Apps","Dynamics 365 apps (Sales, Customer Service) store their data in Dataverse","Integration hub for enterprise data through its Web API and Azure services"],"know":["**Tables, columns, relationships:** it''s relational, even if the UI hides SQL.","**Security roles** and business units control row-level access.","**Delegation-friendly:** works well with Power Apps for large data.","**Premium licensing** is required for Dataverse.","**Solutions** carry tables and customisations between environments."],"analogy":"If SharePoint lists are a shared spreadsheet, Dataverse is a proper filing system with locked cabinets, labelled folders that reference each other, and a log of who opened what.","talk10":"Dataverse is the managed relational database behind the Power Platform. It gives low-code apps proper tables, relationships and role-based security without running a server.","talk30":"Dataverse is Microsoft''s data platform for Power Apps, Power Automate and Dynamics 365. It stores data in relational tables with typed columns and relationships, and adds things a plain database doesn''t give you out of the box: role-based security down to the row, auditing, business rules and a REST API. It''s the natural choice when an app outgrows SharePoint lists, especially if you need relational data, scale or real security. The trade-off is premium licensing.","comebacks":[{"q":"Isn''t it just SQL Server?","a":"It runs on Azure SQL and other storage underneath, but you interact with it as a managed platform with security and business logic, not as a raw database."},{"q":"Why not SharePoint?","a":"SharePoint lists are fine for simple data. Dataverse handles relationships, large volumes and granular security much better."}],"facts":["Dataverse was previously called the Common Data Service (CDS), renamed in 2020.","Dataverse for Teams is a lighter version included with some Microsoft 365 licences."],"deeper":"### Security model in brief\n- **Security roles** grant privileges per table: create, read, write, delete, append, assign, share.\n- **Access levels**: user, business unit, parent-child business unit, organisation.\n- **Teams** and **column-level security** refine access further.\n\n### Logic options, from low-code to pro-code\n1. Business rules (simple form logic)\n2. Calculated and rollup columns, and Power Fx formula columns\n3. Power Automate flows triggered by row changes\n4. Plug-ins written in C# for synchronous, transactional logic","sources":[{"title":"Microsoft Learn: What is Dataverse?","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-apps/maker/data-platform/data-platform-intro"},{"title":"Microsoft Learn: Security concepts in Dataverse","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-platform/admin/wp-security-cds"}],"check":{"prompt":"A manager should see their team''s records but not other departments''. What Dataverse feature handles this best?","options":["Hiding the records with a filter in the app","Security roles with business-unit access levels","A separate environment per department","Column-level security"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Filters in the app only hide data; the API could still return it. Security roles enforce access on the server, and business-unit levels scope it to the manager''s part of the organisation."},"interview":[{"id":"dataverse-iq1","q":"When would you choose Dataverse over SharePoint lists?","level":"intermediate","pro":false,"answer":"1. Relational data with many related tables.\n2. Large volumes where delegation and performance matter.\n3. Granular, role-based or row-level security.\n4. Business logic, auditing and integration needs.\n5. Trade-off: premium licensing cost.","short":"I''d pick Dataverse when the data is relational, large, or needs proper role-based security, like managers seeing only their team''s records. For a small, simple list with basic permissions, SharePoint is cheaper and good enough.","detail":"Bringing up licensing shows practical experience. So does mentioning that security trimming in the UI isn''t real security; Dataverse enforces it server-side.","followups":[{"q":"How does Dataverse security work?","a":"Security roles assign table-level privileges with access levels like user, business unit or organisation, combined with teams and column security."}],"mistakes":["Ignoring licensing in the decision"]},{"id":"dataverse-iq2","q":"Business rules, flows or plug-ins: how do you choose?","level":"advanced","pro":true,"answer":"1. Business rules: simple field logic and validation on forms.\n2. Power Automate: asynchronous processes, notifications and integrations.\n3. Plug-ins: synchronous logic that must run inside the transaction, or complex validation.\n4. Prefer the simplest option that meets the requirement.","short":"I''d use business rules for simple form logic, flows for asynchronous work like notifications or integrations, and C# plug-ins when the logic must run synchronously inside the transaction, like blocking an invalid save.","detail":"The key distinction is synchronous versus asynchronous. If the user must not be able to save invalid data, a flow is too late because it runs after the save.","followups":[{"q":"Why can''t a flow block a save?","a":"Automated flows run asynchronously after the row is saved."}],"mistakes":["Using flows for validation that must block saves"]}]}'::jsonb)
