insert into public.topics (slug, category, title, data) values ('docker', 'backend-infra', 'Docker', '{"slug":"docker","title":"Docker","category":"backend-infra","aliases":["containers","container","Dockerfile","image","docker compose","containerisation","containerization"],"tagline":"Package an app with everything it needs to run, so it behaves the same on every machine.","level":"beginner","minutes":4,"concepts":["docker-containers"],"related":["kubernetes","cloud-computing"],"popular":true,"what":"Docker is a tool for building and running **containers**. A container packages your application together with its runtime, libraries and configuration, and runs it in an isolated environment on a shared operating system kernel. The package is called an **image**; a running instance of an image is a **container**.","why":"\"It works on my machine\" used to be a daily problem. Your laptop has Node 20, the server has Node 18, and a library behaves differently. Virtual machines solved isolation but were heavy and slow to start. Containers give you most of the isolation with a fraction of the overhead, and the same image runs on a laptop, in CI and in production.","how":"You write a **Dockerfile**: step-by-step instructions to build an image (start from a base image, copy code, install dependencies, set the start command). Each step creates a cached **layer**, so rebuilds are fast when only your code changes. At runtime, Linux features called **namespaces** (isolation) and **cgroups** (resource limits) make the process think it has its own machine. Containers are not virtual machines: they share the host''s kernel.","where":["Consistent development environments, often with Docker Compose for app plus database","CI pipelines that build and test in clean, reproducible containers","Deploying services to the cloud, Kubernetes, or platforms like Cloud Run and Azure Container Apps"],"know":["**Image vs container:** an image is the blueprint, a container is a running instance.","**Containers are ephemeral.** Anything written inside is lost when the container is removed, unless you use volumes.","**Layers are cached.** Order your Dockerfile so rarely-changing steps come first.","**One process per container** is the usual convention.","**Containers share the host kernel**, so they''re lighter than VMs but isolate less."],"analogy":"Shipping containers standardised freight: a crane doesn''t care if the box holds bananas or TVs. Docker containers do the same for software: any machine with a container runtime can run any image.","talk10":"Docker packages an app with everything it needs into a container, so it runs the same way on my laptop, in CI and in production.","talk30":"Docker lets you package an application and its dependencies into an image, defined by a Dockerfile, and run it as an isolated container. Unlike a virtual machine, a container shares the host''s operating system kernel, so it starts in seconds and uses little overhead. The main benefit is consistency: the exact same image moves from development to testing to production, which removes a lot of \"works on my machine\" bugs. For running many containers across many servers, you typically add an orchestrator like Kubernetes.","comebacks":[{"q":"Is a container a lightweight VM?","a":"It''s a common shorthand, but technically no. A VM virtualises hardware and runs its own OS; a container is an isolated process sharing the host kernel."},{"q":"Docker vs Kubernetes?","a":"Docker builds and runs containers. Kubernetes runs and manages lots of containers across many machines."}],"facts":["Docker was released in 2013 by a company called dotCloud, which renamed itself Docker, Inc.","Container images follow the OCI (Open Container Initiative) standard, so other tools like Podman can run them."],"deeper":"### A good Dockerfile for Node\n```dockerfile\nFROM node:22-slim\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY package*.json ./\nRUN npm ci --omit=dev\nCOPY . .\nUSER node\nCMD [\"node\", \"server.js\"]\n```\nDependencies are copied and installed before the source code, so editing code doesn''t re-run `npm ci`. Running as a non-root user reduces risk.\n\n### Volumes and networking\n- **Volumes** persist data outside the container''s lifecycle, for example a database''s data directory.\n- Containers on the same Docker network can reach each other by service name.\n\n### Trade-offs\n- Weaker isolation than VMs for untrusted code.\n- Images can get large and carry vulnerable packages; use slim base images and scan them.","sources":[{"title":"Docker docs: What is a container?","url":"https://docs.docker.com/get-started/docker-concepts/the-basics/what-is-a-container/"},{"title":"Docker docs: Dockerfile best practices","url":"https://docs.docker.com/build/building/best-practices/"}],"check":{"prompt":"You restart a database container without a volume, and all the data is gone. Why?","options":["Docker deletes data on every restart","The data lived in the container''s writable layer, which was removed with the container","Databases can''t run in containers","The image was corrupted"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A container''s filesystem changes are lost when the container is removed. Mount a volume for anything that must persist."},"interview":[{"id":"docker-iq1","q":"What is Docker and how is a container different from a VM?","level":"beginner","pro":false,"answer":"1. Docker builds images and runs them as containers.\n2. A container packages an app with its dependencies.\n3. A VM runs a full guest OS on virtualised hardware; a container shares the host kernel.\n4. Containers start faster and use fewer resources; VMs give stronger isolation.","short":"Docker packages an app and its dependencies into a container, so it runs the same everywhere. The difference from a VM is that a VM runs its own whole operating system, while containers share the host''s kernel, so they''re much lighter and start in seconds.","detail":"Mention namespaces and cgroups if they push for depth. Give a real use: \"We run the API and Postgres with Docker Compose locally, and the same API image is deployed to production.\"","followups":[{"q":"What''s the difference between an image and a container?","a":"The image is the read-only template; the container is a running instance with its own writable layer."}],"mistakes":["Saying containers each run their own OS"]},{"id":"docker-iq2","q":"How do you make Docker images smaller and faster to build?","level":"intermediate","pro":true,"answer":"1. Use slim or distroless base images.\n2. Order layers so dependencies install before code is copied.\n3. Use multi-stage builds: build in one stage, copy only the output into a small runtime stage.\n4. Use .dockerignore to exclude node_modules, .git and local files.","short":"I''d use a slim base image, copy the dependency files and install them before copying the source so that layer stays cached, use a multi-stage build so build tools don''t end up in the final image, and add a .dockerignore.","detail":"Mention security as a bonus: smaller images have fewer packages and fewer vulnerabilities, and running as a non-root user.","followups":[{"q":"What is a multi-stage build?","a":"A Dockerfile with several FROM stages. You compile in one and copy only the artifacts into a minimal final image."}],"mistakes":["Copying the whole project before installing dependencies"]}]}'::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug) do update set category = excluded.category, title = excluded.title, data = excluded.data, updated_at = now();
insert into public.topics (slug, category, title, data) values ('git', 'engineering', 'Git', '{"slug":"git","title":"Git","category":"engineering","aliases":["version control","GitHub","branches","merge","rebase","commits","pull request","PR"],"tagline":"The version control system that tracks every change to your code and lets a team work on it at once.","level":"beginner","minutes":4,"concepts":["git-internals"],"related":["system-design","docker"],"popular":true,"what":"Git is a distributed version control system. It records snapshots of your project, called **commits**, so you can see what changed, who changed it and why, go back to any earlier version, and work on different features in parallel using **branches**. GitHub, GitLab and Azure DevOps host Git repositories and add collaboration features like pull requests.","why":"Without version control, teams email zip files, overwrite each other''s work and can''t tell which version is in production. Git gives every developer the full history locally, makes branching cheap, and provides a structured way to combine work.","how":"Each commit is a snapshot of the whole project, plus a pointer to its parent commit, an author and a message. A **branch** is just a movable label pointing at a commit. You make changes, **stage** them, and **commit**. To combine work you **merge** (create a commit joining two histories) or **rebase** (replay your commits on top of another branch). Because it''s distributed, you `push` and `pull` to sync with a shared remote.","where":["Essentially all modern software development","Pull request workflows for code review on GitHub, GitLab and Azure DevOps","CI/CD pipelines that build and deploy on every push","Infrastructure as code and documentation repositories"],"know":["**Commit often, with clear messages.** History is documentation.","**Branches are cheap.** Use a branch per feature or fix.","**Merge vs rebase:** merge preserves history as it happened; rebase creates a cleaner, linear history. Don''t rebase commits others have already pulled.","**Pull requests** are where code review happens.","**`.gitignore`** keeps secrets, build output and dependencies out of the repository."],"analogy":"Git is like save points in a video game, for a whole team. Anyone can create their own save branch, try something risky, and either bring it back into the main story or abandon it, without losing anyone else''s progress.","talk10":"Git is a version control system. It tracks every change to the code as commits, and lets people work on separate branches and merge their work together.","talk30":"Git keeps the full history of a codebase as a series of commits, each a snapshot with an author and message, so you can see what changed and roll back if needed. Branches are lightweight, so each feature or fix gets its own branch, and work comes back together through merges, usually via pull requests where teammates review the changes. It''s distributed, meaning every developer has the full history locally. GitHub and similar platforms host the shared copy and run CI on every push.","comebacks":[{"q":"Git vs GitHub?","a":"Git is the version control tool. GitHub is a hosting service built around Git, with pull requests, issues and CI."},{"q":"Merge or rebase?","a":"Rebase your own local branch to keep history clean; merge shared branches. Never rewrite history others depend on."},{"q":"Can you undo a commit?","a":"Yes. `git revert` creates a new commit that undoes it safely; `git reset` moves the branch back and is for local changes."}],"facts":["Linus Torvalds wrote Git in 2005 for Linux kernel development after the team lost access to their previous tool, BitKeeper.","Git identifies every commit by a hash of its contents, so history can''t be changed without changing the hashes."],"deeper":"### What''s actually stored\nGit stores **blobs** (file contents), **trees** (directories) and **commits** (a tree plus parents and metadata), each addressed by a hash. A branch is a file containing a commit hash. That''s why creating a branch is instant.\n\n### Everyday commands\n```bash\ngit switch -c feature/login   # new branch\ngit add -p                    # stage changes interactively\ngit commit -m \"Add login form validation\"\ngit pull --rebase origin main # update your branch\ngit push -u origin feature/login\n```\n\n### Fixing mistakes\n- Wrong commit message (not pushed): `git commit --amend`\n- Undo a pushed commit safely: `git revert <hash>`\n- Committed a secret: rotate the secret immediately. Removing it from history is not enough, because it may already be copied.","sources":[{"title":"Pro Git book","url":"https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2"},{"title":"Pro Git: Git internals","url":"https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2/Git-Internals-Plumbing-and-Porcelain"}],"check":{"prompt":"You accidentally pushed an API key to a public GitHub repository and then deleted it in a new commit. What should you do?","options":["Nothing, it''s deleted now","Force-push to remove the commit and you''re safe","Revoke and rotate the key immediately, then clean the history","Make the repository private"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Deleting it in a new commit leaves it in history, and bots scan public repositories within minutes. Treat the key as compromised and rotate it."},"interview":[{"id":"git-iq1","q":"What''s the difference between merge and rebase?","level":"intermediate","pro":false,"answer":"1. Merge combines branches with a merge commit, preserving the true history.\n2. Rebase replays your commits on top of another branch, producing a linear history.\n3. Rebase rewrites commit hashes, so don''t rebase shared commits.\n4. Teams often rebase locally and merge (or squash-merge) pull requests.","short":"Merge joins two branches with a merge commit and keeps history exactly as it happened. Rebase moves my commits to sit on top of the latest main, so history is a clean line. Because rebase rewrites commits, I only do it on branches nobody else is using.","detail":"Mention squash merging as a common PR strategy, and that conflicts are resolved in both cases, just at different moments: once for a merge, per commit for a rebase.","followups":[{"q":"What''s a fast-forward merge?","a":"When the target branch hasn''t moved, Git just moves its pointer forward with no merge commit."}],"mistakes":["Rebasing a shared branch and force-pushing over teammates'' work"]},{"id":"git-iq2","q":"How do you resolve a merge conflict?","level":"beginner","pro":false,"answer":"1. A conflict happens when both branches changed the same lines.\n2. Git marks the conflicting sections in the file.\n3. Edit the file to the correct combined result and remove the markers.\n4. Stage the file and complete the merge or rebase.\n5. Run the tests before pushing.","short":"A conflict means two branches changed the same lines. Git marks both versions in the file, I decide what the correct result should be, often talking to whoever made the other change, then stage it, finish the merge and run the tests.","detail":"Showing that you''d talk to the other author when you''re unsure is a good signal. Mention that frequent small merges reduce conflicts.","followups":[{"q":"How do you reduce conflicts?","a":"Small, short-lived branches, pulling from main often, and splitting large files."}],"mistakes":["Accepting one side blindly without understanding the other change"]},{"id":"git-iq3","q":"Describe a Git branching strategy for a team.","level":"advanced","pro":true,"answer":"1. Trunk-based: short-lived feature branches merged into main often, with CI and feature flags.\n2. GitFlow: develop, release and hotfix branches; heavier and suited to versioned releases.\n3. Protect main: required reviews and passing CI before merging.\n4. Choose based on release cadence and team size.","short":"For most teams shipping continuously, I''d use trunk-based development: short-lived branches, pull requests with required reviews and CI, merged into main several times a week, with feature flags for unfinished work. GitFlow makes more sense for products with scheduled, versioned releases.","detail":"Mention branch protection rules and that long-lived branches drift and cause painful merges.","followups":[{"q":"What are feature flags?","a":"Switches in code that let you merge and deploy unfinished features while keeping them turned off for users."}],"mistakes":["Long-lived feature branches without regular syncing"]}]}'::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug) do update set category = excluded.category, title = excluded.title, data = excluded.data, updated_at = now();
insert into public.topics (slug, category, title, data) values ('javascript', 'development', 'JavaScript', '{"slug":"javascript","title":"JavaScript","category":"development","aliases":["JS","ECMAScript","ES6","promises","async await","event loop"],"tagline":"The programming language of the web, running in every browser and, through Node.js, on servers too.","level":"beginner","minutes":5,"concepts":["js-event-loop","nodejs-event-loop"],"related":["typescript","react"],"popular":true,"what":"JavaScript is a dynamically typed programming language that every web browser can run. It started as a way to make web pages interactive. Today it also runs servers (Node.js, Deno, Bun), mobile apps and desktop apps. Its official standard is called ECMAScript, which is why you hear \"ES6\" or \"ES2024\".","why":"In 1995 the web was static documents. Netscape wanted a lightweight language inside the browser so pages could react to clicks and forms without a round trip to the server. Brendan Eich wrote the first version in about ten days. It stuck because it was the only language every browser shipped.","how":"JavaScript runs on a single thread with an **event loop**. Your code runs to completion; slow things like timers, network requests and file reads are handed off to the environment (the browser or Node), and when they finish their callbacks are queued. The event loop picks up the next callback when the call stack is empty. **Promises** and `async/await` are a nicer way to write that same callback-based flow.","where":["Every interactive website: forms, menus, maps, editors","Front-end frameworks: React, Vue, Angular, Svelte","Back ends and APIs with Node.js","Tooling: bundlers, linters and build scripts are mostly JavaScript or TypeScript"],"know":["**Single-threaded, non-blocking.** One thing runs at a time, but waiting doesn''t block. That''s the event loop.","**Dynamic types.** Variables don''t have fixed types, which is flexible and also why TypeScript exists.","**Functions are values.** You can pass them around, return them and store them. Closures come from this.","**`==` vs `===`.** `===` compares without type conversion. Use it by default.","**`let` and `const`** replaced `var` for most code because they''re block-scoped."],"analogy":"The event loop is like a single barista who never stands and waits for the milk to steam. They start the milk, take the next order, and when the steamer beeps they finish that drink. One person, many drinks in progress.","talk10":"JavaScript is the language of the web. It runs in every browser, and with Node.js on servers too. It''s single-threaded but handles lots of waiting work at once through the event loop.","talk30":"JavaScript started as a small scripting language for browsers and became the one language every web page can run. It''s dynamically typed and single-threaded, but it doesn''t block on slow work: network calls and timers are handed off, and their results come back through the event loop as callbacks, promises or async/await. Node.js took the same engine to servers, which is why you can now write a whole product, front end and back end, in JavaScript. TypeScript adds static types on top for larger codebases.","comebacks":[{"q":"Is JavaScript related to Java?","a":"Only by name. It was a marketing decision in 1995. The languages are very different."},{"q":"If it''s single-threaded, how does it handle many requests?","a":"It doesn''t wait on I/O. While a database call is in flight, it serves other work, and the callback runs when the result arrives."},{"q":"Why do people complain about it?","a":"Odd type coercion and some early design decisions. Modern JavaScript and TypeScript fix most of the day-to-day pain."}],"facts":["The language was named Mocha, then LiveScript, before becoming JavaScript in 1995.","`typeof null` returns `\"object\"`. It''s a bug from the first version that can''t be fixed without breaking the web.","ECMAScript has had a new edition every year since 2015."],"deeper":"### Microtasks vs macrotasks\nPromise callbacks go into the **microtask** queue, which drains completely before the next **macrotask** (timers, I/O events). That''s why this prints `sync`, `micro`, `macro`:\n```js\nsetTimeout(() => console.log(''macro''), 0)\nPromise.resolve().then(() => console.log(''micro''))\nconsole.log(''sync'')\n```\n\n### Closures\nA function remembers the variables in scope where it was created. That''s how private state, callbacks and React hooks work.\n```js\nfunction counter() { let n = 0; return () => ++n }\nconst next = counter(); next(); next() // 2\n```\n\n### Trade-offs\n- CPU-heavy work blocks the single thread. Move it to Web Workers or worker threads.\n- Dynamic typing speeds up small scripts and slows down large teams. That gap is where TypeScript fits.","sources":[{"title":"MDN: JavaScript guide","url":"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Guide"},{"title":"MDN: The event loop","url":"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Event_loop"},{"title":"ECMAScript specification","url":"https://tc39.es/ecma262/"}],"check":{"prompt":"What does this log, in order? `setTimeout(() => log(''A''), 0); Promise.resolve().then(() => log(''B'')); log(''C'')`","options":["A, B, C","C, B, A","C, A, B","B, C, A"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Synchronous code runs first (C). Then the microtask queue drains (the promise callback, B). Only then does the event loop take the next macrotask, the timer (A)."},"interview":[{"id":"javascript-iq1","q":"Explain the JavaScript event loop.","level":"intermediate","pro":false,"answer":"1. JavaScript runs on one thread with a call stack.\n2. Async work (timers, network, I/O) is handled by the browser or Node outside the stack.\n3. When that work finishes, its callback is queued.\n4. When the stack is empty, the event loop runs all microtasks (promises) first, then the next macrotask (timers, events).","short":"JavaScript runs one thing at a time on a call stack. Slow work like a fetch is handed off to the browser, and when it''s done the callback gets queued. When the stack is empty, the event loop runs queued promise callbacks first, then things like timers.","detail":"Draw it if you can: call stack, Web APIs, microtask queue, macrotask queue. The classic follow-up is the `setTimeout` vs `Promise.then` ordering puzzle. Saying that `setTimeout(fn, 0)` means \"as soon as possible after the current work and microtasks\", not \"immediately\", shows real understanding.","followups":[{"q":"What''s the difference between microtasks and macrotasks?","a":"Microtasks (promise callbacks, queueMicrotask) all run before the next macrotask (timers, I/O, UI events)."},{"q":"What blocks the event loop?","a":"Long synchronous work, like a big loop or heavy JSON parsing. Nothing else runs until it finishes."}],"mistakes":["Saying JavaScript is multi-threaded","Not distinguishing microtasks from macrotasks"]},{"id":"javascript-iq2","q":"What is a closure?","level":"beginner","pro":false,"answer":"1. A closure is a function together with the variables from the scope where it was defined.\n2. The function can still read and update those variables after the outer function returns.\n3. Uses: private state, factories, callbacks, memoisation, React hooks.","short":"A closure is when a function remembers the variables around it from where it was created, even after that outer function has finished. It''s how you get private state in JavaScript.","detail":"Show a small counter example. A common trap is the old `var` in a loop: every callback shares the same `i`. With `let`, each iteration gets its own binding, so each closure sees its own value.","followups":[{"q":"Why does `var` in a loop with setTimeout print the same number?","a":"`var` is function-scoped, so every callback closes over the same variable, which has its final value by the time they run."}],"mistakes":["Defining closures only as \"a function inside a function\""]},{"id":"javascript-iq3","q":"What''s the difference between == and ===, and between null and undefined?","level":"beginner","pro":true,"answer":"1. `===` checks value and type without conversion. `==` converts types first.\n2. `undefined` means a value was never assigned. `null` means it was intentionally set to \"no value\".\n3. `null == undefined` is true; `null === undefined` is false.\n4. Default to `===`.","short":"Triple equals compares without type conversion, so `''1'' === 1` is false. Double equals converts first, which leads to surprises. Undefined means nothing was assigned, null means someone explicitly said \"empty\".","detail":"One accepted use of `==` is `x == null`, which checks for both null and undefined at once. Mention optional chaining (`a?.b`) and nullish coalescing (`a ?? b`) as modern ways to handle missing values.","followups":[{"q":"What does `??` do differently from `","a":"`?"}],"mistakes":["Saying `typeof null` is \"null\""]}]}'::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug) do update set category = excluded.category, title = excluded.title, data = excluded.data, updated_at = now();
insert into public.topics (slug, category, title, data) values ('kubernetes', 'backend-infra', 'Kubernetes', '{"slug":"kubernetes","title":"Kubernetes","category":"backend-infra","aliases":["K8s","kube","kubectl","pods","container orchestration","AKS","EKS","GKE","Helm"],"tagline":"A system that runs your containers across many machines and keeps them in the state you asked for.","level":"intermediate","minutes":5,"concepts":["docker-containers"],"related":["docker","cloud-computing","system-design"],"popular":true,"what":"Kubernetes, often written K8s, is an open-source **container orchestrator**. You tell it what you want running, for example \"three copies of this API image, reachable at this address\", and it schedules those containers across a cluster of machines, restarts them when they crash and replaces them when you deploy a new version. It came out of Google in 2014.","why":"Running one container is easy. Running 200 containers across 30 servers, with rolling updates, health checks, scaling and networking, is not. Without an orchestrator you end up with scripts and manual work. Kubernetes standardises all of that.","how":"Kubernetes is **declarative**. You write YAML describing the **desired state**, and controllers constantly compare it with the **actual state** and fix any difference. That''s called **reconciliation**. Key objects:\n- **Pod:** the smallest unit, one or more containers that run together.\n- **Deployment:** keeps N replicas of a pod running and handles rolling updates.\n- **Service:** a stable network address that load-balances across pods.\n- **Ingress:** routes external HTTP traffic to services.\n- **ConfigMap and Secret:** configuration and credentials.","where":["Microservice platforms at medium and large companies","Managed offerings: Azure AKS, Amazon EKS, Google GKE","Running data and ML workloads alongside regular services"],"know":["**Declarative desired state** is the core idea. You describe the end state, not the steps.","**Pods are disposable.** They get replaced, rescheduled and get new IPs. Services give stability.","**Self-healing:** crashed containers restart, failed nodes'' pods get rescheduled.","**Horizontal scaling** by changing replicas, manually or with an autoscaler.","**It''s complex.** Many small teams are better off with a simpler managed container service."],"analogy":"Kubernetes is like a thermostat for software. You set \"three copies of the API\", and it keeps checking. If one dies, it starts another. You don''t tell it how; you tell it what.","talk10":"Kubernetes runs containers across a cluster of machines. You declare what you want running, and it keeps it that way: restarting crashed containers, scaling and rolling out updates.","talk30":"Kubernetes is a container orchestrator. Docker packages an app into containers; Kubernetes runs lots of them across many machines. You describe the desired state in YAML, like \"three replicas of this image behind a service\", and Kubernetes continuously reconciles the actual state to match, restarting failed containers, rescheduling them if a machine dies, and doing rolling updates when you deploy. It''s powerful but complex, so smaller teams often use simpler managed platforms unless they really need that control.","comebacks":[{"q":"Do I need Kubernetes?","a":"Only if you''re running many services and need fine control. For one or two apps, a managed platform is usually simpler and cheaper."},{"q":"Is Kubernetes a replacement for Docker?","a":"No. Kubernetes runs containers; images are still built with Docker or similar tools."}],"facts":["\"K8s\" is a numeronym: K, then 8 letters, then s.","Kubernetes is Greek for helmsman, which is why the logo is a ship''s wheel.","It was inspired by Borg, Google''s internal cluster manager."],"deeper":"### A minimal Deployment and Service\n```yaml\napiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata: { name: api }\nspec:\n  replicas: 3\n  selector: { matchLabels: { app: api } }\n  template:\n    metadata: { labels: { app: api } }\n    spec:\n      containers:\n        - name: api\n          image: myrepo/api:1.4.0\n          ports: [{ containerPort: 8080 }]\n          readinessProbe: { httpGet: { path: /health, port: 8080 } }\n---\napiVersion: v1\nkind: Service\nmetadata: { name: api }\nspec:\n  selector: { app: api }\n  ports: [{ port: 80, targetPort: 8080 }]\n```\n\n### Probes\n- **Readiness probe:** \"can this pod receive traffic?\" Failing removes it from the Service.\n- **Liveness probe:** \"is it stuck?\" Failing restarts the container.\n\n### Trade-offs\n- Big operational learning curve: networking, storage, security policies, upgrades.\n- Managed Kubernetes reduces the control-plane work, not the application-level complexity.","sources":[{"title":"Kubernetes docs: Overview","url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/overview/"},{"title":"Kubernetes docs: Deployments","url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/deployment/"}],"check":{"prompt":"A pod in a Deployment with 3 replicas crashes and won''t come back. What does Kubernetes do?","options":["Nothing until an admin intervenes","Creates a new pod to get back to 3 replicas","Shuts down the other 2 pods","Rolls back to the previous image version"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Deployment''s controller sees actual (2) differs from desired (3) and creates a replacement. That''s reconciliation."},"interview":[{"id":"kubernetes-iq1","q":"What is Kubernetes and why use it?","level":"beginner","pro":false,"answer":"1. An open-source container orchestrator.\n2. Schedules containers across a cluster, self-heals and scales.\n3. Declarative: you describe desired state; controllers reconcile actual state.\n4. Handles service discovery, load balancing and rolling updates.","short":"Kubernetes manages containers across many machines. I describe what I want, like three replicas of a service, and it keeps that true: restarting failures, scaling and rolling out new versions without downtime.","detail":"Mention when you wouldn''t use it. Saying \"for a small app I''d pick a managed container service\" shows judgment, which interviewers value more than buzzwords.","followups":[{"q":"What is a pod?","a":"The smallest deployable unit: one or more containers sharing a network and storage, scheduled together."},{"q":"What''s a Service?","a":"A stable address that load-balances traffic to a changing set of pods selected by labels."}],"mistakes":["Confusing pods and containers"]},{"id":"kubernetes-iq2","q":"How does a rolling update work in Kubernetes?","level":"intermediate","pro":true,"answer":"1. You change the Deployment''s image.\n2. Kubernetes creates new pods with the new version, a few at a time.\n3. New pods receive traffic only after passing readiness probes.\n4. Old pods are terminated gradually; maxSurge and maxUnavailable control the pace.\n5. `kubectl rollout undo` rolls back.","short":"When I update the image, the Deployment brings up new pods gradually and only sends them traffic once their readiness probe passes, then removes old pods. If something goes wrong, I can roll back to the previous version.","detail":"Without readiness probes, traffic can hit pods that haven''t finished starting, which causes errors during deploys. Mention that database migrations need to be backward compatible because old and new versions run at the same time.","followups":[{"q":"Why must migrations be backward compatible?","a":"During a rolling update, old and new versions of the app run simultaneously against the same database."}],"mistakes":["Forgetting readiness probes"]}]}'::jsonb)
