insert into public.topics (slug, category, title, data) values ('llms', 'ai', 'Large Language Models', '{"slug":"llms","title":"Large Language Models","category":"ai","aliases":["LLM","GPT","Claude","Gemini","Llama","transformers","tokens","generative AI","GenAI"],"tagline":"Models trained on huge amounts of text to predict the next token, which turns out to be enough to write, summarise and reason.","level":"beginner","minutes":5,"concepts":["llm-tokens","embeddings-vector-db","rag"],"related":["rag","vector-databases"],"popular":true,"what":"A large language model (LLM) is a neural network trained on a huge amount of text to predict the next piece of text, called a **token**. GPT, Claude, Gemini and Llama are LLMs. Given a prompt, the model generates a response one token at a time, each time choosing a likely next token based on everything so far.","why":"Classic NLP needed a separate model for each task: one for translation, one for sentiment, one for summarising. Researchers found that one big model trained to predict text gets surprisingly good at all of those, and can follow instructions it was never explicitly trained on. That generality is why LLMs took off.","how":"Text is split into **tokens** (pieces of words). The model, usually a **transformer**, turns each token into numbers and uses **attention** to weigh how every token relates to every other token in the context. It outputs a probability for each possible next token, picks one, adds it to the input and repeats. Training happens in stages: **pre-training** on large text datasets, then **fine-tuning** and human feedback to make it helpful and follow instructions.","where":["Chat assistants and coding assistants","Summarising documents, meetings and support tickets","Extracting structured data from emails, invoices and contracts","Search and Q&A over company documents, usually with RAG","Agents that call tools and APIs to complete tasks"],"know":["**It predicts tokens; it doesn''t look things up.** That''s why it can sound confident and be wrong (hallucination).","**Context window:** the maximum tokens it can consider at once, including your prompt and its answer.","**Knowledge cutoff:** it only knows what was in its training data, unless you give it more in the prompt.","**Temperature** controls randomness. Low is more predictable, high is more varied.","**Cost and speed scale with tokens.** Long prompts cost more and take longer."],"analogy":"An LLM is like an extremely well-read improviser. It has absorbed patterns from a vast library and can continue almost any text convincingly. It isn''t reading from the library while it speaks, so it can misremember a fact as fluently as it recalls one.","talk10":"An LLM is a model trained on huge amounts of text to predict the next token. Doing that well turns out to let it write, summarise, translate and answer questions, though it can confidently get facts wrong.","talk30":"Large language models are neural networks, mostly transformers, trained to predict the next token in a piece of text. They''re trained on huge datasets and then fine-tuned to follow instructions, which makes them useful for writing, summarising, coding and extracting information. The important limitation is that they generate plausible text rather than looking facts up, so they can hallucinate, and they only know what was in their training data. That''s why products often add retrieval, like RAG, or tools, to ground the answers in real data.","comebacks":[{"q":"Does it understand what it''s saying?","a":"That''s debated. Practically, it''s very good at using language in context, but it has no built-in way to check facts against reality."},{"q":"Why does it make things up?","a":"It''s trained to produce likely text, not true text. When it doesn''t know, a fluent guess is often the most likely continuation."},{"q":"How do companies use it on their own data?","a":"Usually by retrieving relevant documents and putting them in the prompt (RAG), not by retraining the model."}],"facts":["The transformer architecture comes from Google''s 2017 paper \"Attention Is All You Need\".","A token is roughly three-quarters of an English word on average, and languages like Hindi often use more tokens per word.","Models don''t see letters directly, which is why they used to struggle counting letters in a word."],"deeper":"### Attention, simply\nFor each token, attention computes how relevant every other token in the context is, and mixes in their information accordingly. In \"the animal didn''t cross the street because it was tired\", attention helps the model link \"it\" to \"animal\". Stacking many attention layers lets the model build up increasingly abstract relationships.\n\n### Training stages\n1. **Pre-training:** predict the next token over a huge corpus. This is where general knowledge and language skill come from.\n2. **Instruction tuning:** train on examples of prompts and good responses.\n3. **Preference tuning:** methods like RLHF use human or AI feedback to prefer helpful, honest, harmless responses.\n\n### Trade-offs when building with LLMs\n- **Accuracy vs cost:** bigger models are usually better and slower and more expensive.\n- **Prompting vs RAG vs fine-tuning:** prompting is cheapest to try, RAG adds fresh or private knowledge, fine-tuning changes style or behaviour.\n- **Evaluation is hard:** you need test sets and checks, not just a few good-looking demos.","sources":[{"title":"Vaswani et al., Attention Is All You Need (2017)","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.03762"},{"title":"OpenAI: What are tokens?","url":"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/4936856-what-are-tokens-and-how-to-count-them"},{"title":"Anthropic: Prompt engineering overview","url":"https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/build-with-claude/prompt-engineering/overview"}],"check":{"prompt":"A model confidently cites a policy document that doesn''t exist. What''s the best description of why?","options":["The model''s database was corrupted","It generates likely-sounding text and has no built-in fact lookup","The temperature was set to zero","The context window was too large"],"answer":1,"explanation":"LLMs generate plausible continuations. Unless the real document is in the prompt (for example via RAG), a convincing fake is a likely output. That''s hallucination."},"interview":[{"id":"llms-iq1","q":"What is a large language model?","level":"beginner","pro":false,"answer":"1. A neural network, usually a transformer, trained to predict the next token in text.\n2. Trained on huge text datasets, then fine-tuned to follow instructions.\n3. Generates output one token at a time.\n4. Limits: hallucination, knowledge cutoff, context window, cost per token.","short":"An LLM is a model trained on a huge amount of text to predict the next token. That simple objective, at scale, gives it the ability to write, summarise and answer questions. The catch is that it predicts plausible text rather than checking facts.","detail":"Mention a concrete limitation and how you''d handle it: \"For a support bot, I wouldn''t trust the model''s memory of our policies. I''d retrieve the actual policy text and ask it to answer only from that.\" That shows you can build with it, not just describe it.","followups":[{"q":"What''s a token?","a":"A chunk of text, often part of a word. Models read and write tokens, and pricing and context limits are measured in them."},{"q":"What''s a context window?","a":"The maximum number of tokens the model can take into account at once, prompt plus output."}],"mistakes":["Saying the model searches the internet by default","Ignoring hallucination entirely"]},{"id":"llms-iq2","q":"How would you reduce hallucinations in an LLM feature?","level":"intermediate","pro":false,"answer":"1. Ground it: put real source content in the prompt (RAG) and ask it to answer only from that.\n2. Ask for citations, and let it say \"I don''t know\".\n3. Lower the temperature for factual tasks.\n4. Validate structured output and check claims where possible.\n5. Evaluate with a test set of real questions.","short":"The main fix is grounding: retrieve the actual documents and tell the model to answer only from them, with citations, and to say when it doesn''t know. Then I''d measure it with a set of real questions rather than trusting a demo.","detail":"Interviewers like to hear that this is an engineering problem with measurement, not just a prompt trick. Mention retrieval quality as the usual bottleneck, and human review for high-stakes outputs.","followups":[{"q":"Would fine-tuning fix hallucinations?","a":"Not reliably. Fine-tuning shapes behaviour and style. Grounding with retrieval is the better tool for factual accuracy."}],"mistakes":["Claiming hallucinations can be eliminated entirely"]},{"id":"llms-iq3","q":"When would you fine-tune instead of using RAG?","level":"advanced","pro":true,"answer":"1. RAG: when the model needs fresh, private or frequently changing knowledge.\n2. Fine-tuning: when you need a consistent style, format or behaviour, or a narrow task done cheaply by a smaller model.\n3. Often both: fine-tune for behaviour, RAG for knowledge.\n4. Start with prompting, then RAG, then fine-tuning, in increasing cost.","short":"RAG is for knowledge: it gives the model the right facts at question time, and you can update them without retraining. Fine-tuning is for behaviour: a consistent format, tone or a narrow task. I''d start with prompting, add RAG for knowledge, and fine-tune only when there''s a clear behavioural gap.","detail":"Mention cost and maintenance: fine-tuned models need retraining when data changes, and you need good training examples. RAG needs a retrieval pipeline and evaluation of search quality.","followups":[{"q":"What data do you need to fine-tune?","a":"Many high-quality example inputs and ideal outputs for the exact task."}],"mistakes":["Treating fine-tuning as the way to \"add documents\" to a model"]}]}'::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug) do update set category = excluded.category, title = excluded.title, data = excluded.data, updated_at = now();
insert into public.topics (slug, category, title, data) values ('postgresql', 'data-cloud', 'PostgreSQL', '{"slug":"postgresql","title":"PostgreSQL","category":"data-cloud","aliases":["Postgres","SQL","relational database","RDBMS","psql","Supabase","indexes","MVCC"],"tagline":"The open-source relational database that most new products reach for first.","level":"beginner","minutes":5,"concepts":["sql-indexes","postgres-mvcc","supabase-rls"],"related":["vector-databases","system-design","cloud-computing"],"popular":true,"what":"PostgreSQL, usually called Postgres, is an open-source relational database. It stores data in tables with rows and columns, you query it with SQL, and it guarantees that transactions either fully happen or don''t happen at all. It''s been developed since the 1980s and is known for reliability and a huge extension ecosystem.","why":"Applications need to store data so that it survives crashes, stays consistent when many users write at once, and can be queried flexibly. Relational databases solved that decades ago. Postgres became the default open-source choice because it''s free, standards-compliant, and keeps adding features like JSON support, full-text search and vector search.","how":"You define tables with types and constraints, and relate them with foreign keys. SQL queries are parsed, planned by a **query planner** that picks the cheapest way to get the data (for example, using an index or scanning the table), and executed. **Transactions** follow ACID rules. Postgres uses **MVCC** (multi-version concurrency control), so readers don''t block writers: each transaction sees a consistent snapshot.","where":["Back ends for web and mobile apps (Instagram, Reddit and many startups have used it)","Managed platforms: Supabase, Neon, AWS RDS and Aurora, Google Cloud SQL, Azure","Analytics on moderate data sizes, geospatial apps with PostGIS, and vector search with pgvector"],"know":["**Relational model:** data lives in tables, and you join them by keys.","**ACID transactions:** atomic, consistent, isolated, durable.","**Indexes** make reads fast and writes a little slower. Most slow queries are a missing index.","**`EXPLAIN ANALYZE`** shows how a query actually ran.","**JSONB** lets you store flexible documents alongside relational data.","**Row Level Security (RLS)** lets the database itself enforce who can see which rows."],"analogy":"Postgres is a very strict, very fast librarian. Every book has a fixed catalogue card format, nothing gets shelved half-done, and the card index (your indexes) means they don''t have to walk every aisle to find what you asked for.","talk10":"Postgres is an open-source relational database: tables, SQL and reliable transactions. It''s the default choice for a lot of new apps because it''s robust and has extensions for things like JSON, geospatial and vector search.","talk30":"PostgreSQL is a relational database, so data lives in tables you query with SQL, and every transaction is all-or-nothing. It''s open source, very reliable, and has grown a lot of features: JSONB for document-style data, full-text search, PostGIS for maps, and pgvector for AI embeddings. It handles concurrency with MVCC, so reads don''t block writes. Most performance work comes down to good schema design, the right indexes and reading query plans with EXPLAIN ANALYZE. Platforms like Supabase and Neon make it easy to use as a managed service.","comebacks":[{"q":"SQL or NoSQL?","a":"For most apps with related data, start with Postgres. JSONB covers a lot of \"I need flexible fields\" cases. NoSQL makes sense for specific scale or access patterns."},{"q":"Is Postgres slow at scale?","a":"Large companies run it at big scale. Usually slowness is missing indexes or poor queries, not Postgres itself."},{"q":"What''s Supabase?","a":"A hosted Postgres with auth, storage and auto-generated APIs on top."}],"facts":["Postgres grew out of the POSTGRES project at UC Berkeley, started in 1986 by Michael Stonebraker.","It''s been the \"DBMS of the Year\" on DB-Engines several times.","Deleted and updated rows aren''t removed immediately because of MVCC. VACUUM cleans them up later."],"deeper":"### Indexes, briefly\nThe default index is a **B-tree**, good for equality and range lookups. A composite index on `(user_id, created_at)` serves `where user_id = ? order by created_at` well, but not a query filtering only on `created_at`. Column order matters.\n\n### Reading a plan\n```sql\nexplain analyze select * from orders where customer_id = 42;\n```\nA `Seq Scan` on a large table for a selective filter usually means a missing index. An `Index Scan` or `Bitmap Index Scan` is what you want.\n\n### Isolation levels\nPostgres defaults to **Read Committed**. **Repeatable Read** and **Serializable** give stronger guarantees at the cost of possible serialization errors that your code must retry.","sources":[{"title":"PostgreSQL documentation","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/"},{"title":"PostgreSQL docs: Using EXPLAIN","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/using-explain.html"},{"title":"PostgreSQL docs: MVCC","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/mvcc-intro.html"}],"check":{"prompt":"A query `where email = ''a@b.com''` on a 5-million-row users table is slow. The plan shows a Seq Scan. What''s the likely fix?","options":["Increase the server''s RAM","Add an index on email","Switch to a NoSQL database","Use SELECT with fewer columns"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A sequential scan reads every row. An index on `email` (often unique) lets Postgres jump straight to the matching row."},"interview":[{"id":"postgresql-iq1","q":"What is ACID?","level":"beginner","pro":false,"answer":"1. Atomicity: a transaction fully happens or not at all.\n2. Consistency: constraints hold before and after each transaction.\n3. Isolation: concurrent transactions don''t see each other''s partial work.\n4. Durability: once committed, data survives a crash.","short":"ACID describes reliable transactions. Atomic means all or nothing, consistent means rules like constraints always hold, isolated means concurrent transactions don''t step on each other, and durable means committed data survives crashes. A bank transfer is the classic example.","detail":"Use the transfer example: debit one account and credit another in one transaction, so a crash can''t leave money missing. Mention that isolation has levels, which trade strictness for concurrency.","followups":[{"q":"What are isolation levels?","a":"Read Committed, Repeatable Read and Serializable. Higher levels prevent more anomalies but can cause more retries."}],"mistakes":["Mixing up the consistency in ACID with the consistency in the CAP theorem"]},{"id":"postgresql-iq2","q":"How do indexes work and what are their costs?","level":"intermediate","pro":false,"answer":"1. An index is a separate data structure, usually a B-tree, that maps column values to rows.\n2. It turns full table scans into fast lookups for selective queries.\n3. Costs: extra storage, and slower inserts and updates because the index must be maintained.\n4. Composite index column order matters.","short":"An index is like a book''s index: a sorted structure that lets the database jump straight to matching rows instead of scanning the whole table. The cost is extra storage and slower writes, so you add indexes for the queries you actually run.","detail":"Mention checking with EXPLAIN ANALYZE, the leftmost-prefix rule for composite indexes, and that low-selectivity columns, like a boolean, often don''t benefit.","followups":[{"q":"When won''t Postgres use your index?","a":"When the query returns a large share of the table, when a function wraps the column, or when the query doesn''t match the index''s leading column."}],"mistakes":["Saying \"index every column\""]},{"id":"postgresql-iq3","q":"What is MVCC?","level":"advanced","pro":true,"answer":"1. Multi-Version Concurrency Control keeps multiple versions of a row.\n2. Each transaction sees a consistent snapshot of the data.\n3. Readers don''t block writers and writers don''t block readers.\n4. Old versions are cleaned up later by VACUUM.","short":"MVCC means Postgres keeps old versions of rows around, so each transaction reads a consistent snapshot. That way reads and writes don''t block each other. The trade-off is cleanup: VACUUM has to remove dead row versions.","detail":"Mention that an UPDATE in Postgres writes a new row version rather than overwriting in place, which is why heavy updates cause table bloat, and why long-running transactions prevent cleanup.","followups":[{"q":"What is table bloat?","a":"Space taken by dead row versions that haven''t been vacuumed yet."}],"mistakes":["Thinking MVCC means no locks at all; writers still lock rows against other writers"]}]}'::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug) do update set category = excluded.category, title = excluded.title, data = excluded.data, updated_at = now();
insert into public.topics (slug, category, title, data) values ('power-apps', 'enterprise', 'Power Apps', '{"slug":"power-apps","title":"Power Apps","category":"enterprise","aliases":["PowerApps","canvas apps","model-driven apps","Power Fx","Power Platform","low-code"],"tagline":"Microsoft''s low-code tool for building business apps on top of your organisation''s data.","level":"beginner","minutes":4,"concepts":["power-apps-delegation","dataverse"],"related":["power-automate","dataverse"],"popular":true,"what":"Power Apps is Microsoft''s low-code platform for building business applications. You build screens with drag-and-drop controls and write logic in **Power Fx**, an Excel-like formula language. Apps connect to data in Dataverse, SharePoint, SQL Server, Excel and hundreds of other services through **connectors**. It''s part of the Microsoft Power Platform.","why":"Every organisation has processes run on spreadsheets and email: leave requests, asset tracking, site inspections. Building a custom app for each one used to need developers and months. Power Apps lets people who know the process build a working app in days, inside the Microsoft 365 security and identity they already use.","how":"There are two main types:\n- **Canvas apps:** you design every screen freely, like a slide deck, and wire controls to data with formulas. Good for task-focused and mobile apps.\n- **Model-driven apps:** you model your data in Dataverse, and the app''s forms, views and navigation are generated from it. Good for data-heavy back-office apps.\nFormulas are reactive, like Excel: if a label shows `Gallery1.Selected.Name`, it updates automatically when the selection changes.","where":["Internal business apps: approvals, inspections, inventory, HR requests","Field-worker apps on phones and tablets, including offline scenarios","Front ends over SharePoint lists or Dataverse tables","Teams apps for departments"],"know":["**Canvas vs model-driven** is the first decision.","**Delegation:** for large data sources, filters must be \"delegable\" so the server does the work. Otherwise Power Apps only processes the first 500 (configurable up to 2,000) rows and silently misses the rest.","**Licensing matters:** premium connectors and Dataverse need premium licences.","**Power Fx** is declarative and reactive, like Excel formulas.","**Environments and ALM:** use solutions and separate dev, test and production environments."],"analogy":"Power Apps is like building with LEGO Technic instead of machining every part. You get standard pieces that fit together, which is much faster, as long as you design within what the pieces can do.","talk10":"Power Apps is Microsoft''s low-code platform for business apps. You build screens with drag-and-drop and Excel-like formulas, connected to data like SharePoint or Dataverse.","talk30":"Power Apps lets you build internal business apps quickly, using drag-and-drop screens and Power Fx, a formula language that works a lot like Excel. Canvas apps give you full control over the layout, while model-driven apps are generated from your Dataverse data model. Apps connect to hundreds of data sources through connectors and run inside Microsoft 365 security. The main technical thing to understand is delegation: with large data sources, your filters need to run on the server, or the app silently works on only the first few hundred rows.","comebacks":[{"q":"Is low-code real development?","a":"It''s development with different trade-offs. You still need data modelling, performance thinking and deployment discipline, just less boilerplate."},{"q":"When shouldn''t you use Power Apps?","a":"Public, consumer-facing apps, very complex UIs, or heavy real-time processing are better built with traditional code."},{"q":"SharePoint or Dataverse?","a":"SharePoint lists are fine for simple, small apps. Dataverse is better for relational data, security roles and scale."}],"facts":["Power Fx was open-sourced by Microsoft in 2021.","The default delegation limit is 500 rows and can be raised to 2,000."],"deeper":"### Delegation, concretely\n```text\nFilter(Orders, Status = \"Open\")         // delegable to Dataverse: server filters\nFilter(Orders, Len(Notes) > 100)        // may not be delegable: only first N rows checked\n```\nPower Apps shows a blue underline and a warning for non-delegable formulas. Ignoring it is the most common cause of \"the app can''t find records that definitely exist\".\n\n### ALM basics\n- Build in a **solution**, not loose in an environment.\n- Use **environment variables** and **connection references** so the same solution works in dev, test and production.\n- Use pipelines to promote managed solutions to production.\n\n### Performance tips\n- Load only the columns and rows you need.\n- Use `Concurrent()` for independent data loads at app start.\n- Avoid heavy logic in `OnStart`; prefer named formulas and loading data per screen.","sources":[{"title":"Microsoft Learn: What is Power Apps?","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-apps/powerapps-overview"},{"title":"Microsoft Learn: Understand delegation in a canvas app","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-apps/maker/canvas-apps/delegation-overview"},{"title":"Microsoft Learn: Power Fx overview","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-platform/power-fx/overview"}],"check":{"prompt":"A canvas app searching a 20,000-row SharePoint list can''t find items users know exist. The formula shows a delegation warning. What''s happening?","options":["SharePoint is down","The filter runs locally on only the first 500–2,000 rows","The user lacks permission to every row","Power Apps caches stale data"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Non-delegable formulas pull a limited number of rows and filter them on the device. Records beyond that limit are never checked. Rewrite the filter with delegable functions."},"interview":[{"id":"power-apps-iq1","q":"What''s the difference between canvas and model-driven apps?","level":"beginner","pro":false,"answer":"1. Canvas: you design every screen freely; connect to many data sources.\n2. Model-driven: UI is generated from the Dataverse data model; consistent, data-heavy.\n3. Canvas suits task-based and mobile apps; model-driven suits complex back-office processes.","short":"With canvas apps I design every screen myself and can connect to almost any data source, which is great for focused or mobile apps. Model-driven apps are built on Dataverse, and the forms and views come from the data model, which suits larger back-office systems with lots of related data.","detail":"Mention that both can live in the same solution, and that you''d choose based on the users'' task, the data complexity and licensing.","followups":[{"q":"Can a canvas app use Dataverse?","a":"Yes. Canvas apps can use Dataverse as a data source too."}],"mistakes":["Saying model-driven apps can''t be customised at all"]},{"id":"power-apps-iq2","q":"What is delegation in Power Apps?","level":"intermediate","pro":false,"answer":"1. Delegation means the data source does the filtering and sorting, not the app.\n2. Non-delegable formulas only process the first 500 (up to 2,000) records locally.\n3. This leads to silently incomplete results on large data.\n4. Fix: use delegable functions and operators for the data source, or restructure the data.","short":"Delegation is whether Power Apps can push a query to the data source. If a formula isn''t delegable, the app downloads only the first 500 to 2,000 records and filters those, so results on big lists are silently incomplete. I watch for the delegation warnings and rewrite filters to be delegable.","detail":"Give a real example: `StartsWith` is delegable to SharePoint for text columns, while `Search` and `in` often are not. Mention that delegation support differs by data source, so you check the documentation for the specific connector.","followups":[{"q":"How would you handle a search that isn''t delegable?","a":"Pre-filter with a delegable condition, add an indexed column, or move the query to Dataverse or a flow."}],"mistakes":["Fixing it by just raising the limit to 2,000"]},{"id":"power-apps-iq3","q":"How do you manage ALM for Power Apps?","level":"advanced","pro":true,"answer":"1. Build everything inside solutions.\n2. Separate dev, test and production environments.\n3. Use environment variables and connection references for environment-specific settings.\n4. Deploy managed solutions to test and production with pipelines or Azure DevOps or GitHub Actions.\n5. Source control the unpacked solution.","short":"I keep everything in solutions, develop in a dev environment and deploy managed solutions to test and production through pipelines. Environment variables and connection references let the same solution point at the right data in each environment.","detail":"Mention why managed solutions matter in production: they prevent unmanaged edits that drift from source control.","followups":[{"q":"Managed vs unmanaged solution?","a":"Unmanaged is for development and editable. Managed is a locked, versioned package for deployment."}],"mistakes":["Building directly in production"]}]}'::jsonb)
