insert into public.topics (slug, category, title, data) values ('power-automate', 'enterprise', 'Power Automate', '{"slug":"power-automate","title":"Power Automate","category":"enterprise","aliases":["Microsoft Flow","flows","cloud flows","desktop flows","RPA","workflow automation","Power Platform"],"tagline":"Microsoft''s tool for automating workflows between apps and services, triggered by events, schedules or buttons.","level":"beginner","minutes":4,"concepts":["dataverse"],"related":["power-apps","dataverse","rest-apis"],"popular":true,"what":"Power Automate, formerly Microsoft Flow, is Microsoft''s workflow automation tool. A **flow** starts with a **trigger** (an email arrives, a file is created, a form is submitted, a schedule fires) and then runs a series of **actions** (send an email, create a folder, update a record, post to Teams, call an API). It connects to hundreds of services through connectors.","why":"A lot of office work is copying information from one place to another: download the attachment, save it to SharePoint, notify the manager, update the tracker. It''s repetitive and easy to get wrong. Power Automate turns those steps into a flow that runs every time, the same way.","how":"- **Cloud flows** run in Microsoft''s cloud. They can be **automated** (event triggers), **instant** (button or Power Apps triggers) or **scheduled**.\n- **Desktop flows** do RPA (robotic process automation): they click through desktop apps and old systems that have no API.\nFlows use **expressions** for logic (for example `formatDateTime()` or `concat()`), conditions, loops (`Apply to each`) and approvals.","where":["Approvals: leave, purchase orders, document sign-off","Notifications: alert a Teams channel when a ticket is created","Data sync: copy form responses into SharePoint or Dataverse","File handling: save email attachments, create folders by project or status","Calling REST APIs from line-of-business systems"],"know":["**Trigger plus actions** is the whole mental model.","**Run history** shows each run''s inputs and outputs, which is the main debugging tool.","**Error handling:** use \"Configure run after\" and Scope actions to build try-catch patterns.","**Throttling and limits:** connectors have request limits; loops over thousands of items get slow.","**Ownership:** flows owned by one person break when they leave. Use service accounts or solutions."],"analogy":"Power Automate is a rule-following assistant: \"whenever this happens, do these things, in this order.\" They never forget a step, and they do exactly what you wrote, even when you wrote it wrong.","talk10":"Power Automate is Microsoft''s workflow automation tool. You set a trigger, like a new email or form submission, and it runs actions across apps automatically.","talk30":"Power Automate lets you automate workflows across Microsoft 365 and hundreds of other services. Every flow has a trigger, like a new item in a SharePoint list, a form submission or a schedule, followed by actions like sending emails, creating files, updating records or starting an approval. Cloud flows work through APIs and connectors, and desktop flows can automate old desktop apps through RPA. In real projects, the important parts are error handling, staying within connector limits, and making sure flows don''t depend on one person''s account.","comebacks":[{"q":"Is it the same as Zapier?","a":"Similar idea. Power Automate is deeply integrated with Microsoft 365, Dataverse and Azure AD identity, which is why enterprises on Microsoft use it."},{"q":"What''s RPA?","a":"Robotic process automation: software that clicks and types through a user interface, for systems without an API."},{"q":"Can it call any API?","a":"Yes, using the HTTP action (premium) or a custom connector."}],"facts":["Microsoft Flow was renamed Power Automate in November 2019.","The HTTP action and many connectors like SQL Server are premium, which affects licensing."],"deeper":"### Error handling pattern\n1. Put the main steps in a **Scope** called Try.\n2. Add a second Scope called Catch, set to \"run after\" Try **has failed** or **has timed out**.\n3. In Catch, log the error (`result(''Try'')`) and notify someone.\n\n### Performance\n- Filter at the source with OData filter queries instead of getting all items and filtering in a loop.\n- Turn on **concurrency** for `Apply to each` when order doesn''t matter.\n- Avoid nested loops over large lists.\n\n### Working with expressions\n```text\nformatDateTime(utcNow(), ''yyyy-MM-dd'')\nconcat(''Projects/'', triggerBody()?[''Status''], ''/'', triggerBody()?[''Title''])\n```","sources":[{"title":"Microsoft Learn: Power Automate documentation","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-automate/"},{"title":"Microsoft Learn: Limits and configuration","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-automate/limits-and-config"},{"title":"Microsoft Learn: Error handling in flows","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-automate/guidance/coding-guidelines/error-handling"}],"check":{"prompt":"A flow loops over 5,000 SharePoint items to find 20 with Status = \"Overdue\". It''s slow. What''s the best fix?","options":["Add a delay inside the loop","Use a filter query in Get items so SharePoint returns only the 20","Split the flow into five flows","Switch to a desktop flow"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Filtering at the source means only the needed items come back. Looping over everything wastes time and API calls."},"interview":[{"id":"power-automate-iq1","q":"What is Power Automate and what types of flows are there?","level":"beginner","pro":false,"answer":"1. Microsoft''s workflow automation service in the Power Platform.\n2. Cloud flows: automated (event), instant (manual or from Power Apps), scheduled.\n3. Desktop flows: RPA for UI-based automation.\n4. Business process flows guide users through stages in model-driven apps.","short":"Power Automate automates workflows between services. Cloud flows run on triggers, buttons or schedules, and desktop flows use RPA to automate apps without APIs. I''ve used it for things like approvals and notifications.","detail":"If you have built one, describe it concretely: the trigger, the main actions and how you handled failures. That''s much stronger than listing flow types.","followups":[{"q":"How do you debug a failed flow?","a":"Open the run history, check each action''s inputs and outputs, and find the first failed step."}],"mistakes":["Not mentioning error handling at all"]},{"id":"power-automate-iq2","q":"How do you make flows reliable in production?","level":"intermediate","pro":true,"answer":"1. Try-catch with Scopes and \"Configure run after\".\n2. Notifications or logging on failure.\n3. Build in solutions with connection references; use service accounts, not personal accounts.\n4. Respect limits: filter at source, use pagination, concurrency where safe.\n5. Make actions safe to re-run where possible.","short":"I wrap the main logic in a Try scope with a Catch scope that runs on failure and alerts someone. Flows live in solutions with connection references and run under a service account, so they don''t break when someone leaves. And I filter data at the source to stay within limits.","detail":"Mention that triggers can fire more than once in some situations, so actions like creating records should check for duplicates.","followups":[{"q":"Why avoid personal connections?","a":"If that person leaves or changes their password, every flow using their connection stops."}],"mistakes":["Owning critical flows with a personal account"]}]}'::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug) do update set category = excluded.category, title = excluded.title, data = excluded.data, updated_at = now();
insert into public.topics (slug, category, title, data) values ('rag', 'ai', 'RAG', '{"slug":"rag","title":"RAG","category":"ai","aliases":["Retrieval-Augmented Generation","retrieval augmented generation","chat with your docs","grounding"],"tagline":"Give a language model the right documents at question time, so it answers from your data instead of its memory.","level":"intermediate","minutes":5,"concepts":["rag","embeddings-vector-db","llm-tokens"],"related":["llms","vector-databases"],"popular":true,"what":"RAG stands for **Retrieval-Augmented Generation**. When a user asks a question, the system first searches your documents for the most relevant passages, puts them into the prompt, and then asks the LLM to answer using those passages. The model doesn''t learn anything new. It''s reading from notes you handed it.","why":"LLMs only know their training data, which is frozen at a cutoff date and doesn''t include your company''s policies, tickets or wiki. Retraining a model every time a document changes is slow and expensive. RAG lets you keep the model as-is and swap the knowledge in at question time.","how":"1. **Index:** split your documents into chunks, turn each chunk into an **embedding** (a vector of numbers capturing its meaning) and store them in a vector database.\n2. **Retrieve:** embed the user''s question the same way and find the chunks with the closest vectors, often combined with keyword search.\n3. **Augment:** put the top chunks into the prompt with instructions like \"answer only from this context and cite sources\".\n4. **Generate:** the LLM writes the answer.","where":["Internal \"ask the docs\" assistants for HR policies, engineering wikis and runbooks","Customer support bots that answer from help-centre articles","Search over contracts, research papers or legal documents","Coding assistants that pull in relevant files from your repository"],"know":["**Retrieval quality caps answer quality.** If the right chunk isn''t retrieved, the model can''t use it.","**Chunking matters:** too small loses context, too big dilutes relevance and wastes tokens.","**Hybrid search** (vectors plus keywords) usually beats either alone, especially for names, codes and IDs.","**Citations** make answers checkable and build trust.","**Evaluate it:** keep a set of real questions with known good answers and measure retrieval and answer quality."],"analogy":"RAG is an open-book exam. The model is a student who writes well, and retrieval is finding the right pages to bring in. A brilliant student with the wrong pages still writes a confident, wrong answer.","talk10":"RAG means retrieving relevant documents and adding them to the prompt, so the LLM answers from your actual data instead of just its training memory.","talk30":"RAG, retrieval-augmented generation, is how most companies make an LLM answer questions about their own data. You split documents into chunks, store embeddings of those chunks in a vector database, and at question time you find the chunks most similar to the question and put them in the prompt. The model then answers from that context, ideally with citations. It doesn''t retrain the model, so you can update the knowledge just by updating the documents. In practice, most of the work is in getting retrieval right, because the model can only be as good as what you give it.","comebacks":[{"q":"Why not just fine-tune on our documents?","a":"Fine-tuning is good at teaching style and behaviour, not reliable at memorising facts, and you''d have to retrain whenever documents change. RAG updates instantly."},{"q":"Why not paste all the docs into the prompt?","a":"Context windows and costs are limited, and models handle focused context better than a giant dump."},{"q":"Does RAG stop hallucinations?","a":"It reduces them a lot when retrieval works. It doesn''t eliminate them, so citations and evaluation still matter."}],"facts":["The term comes from a 2020 paper by researchers at Facebook AI Research (Lewis et al.).","Many production RAG systems add a **re-ranker**, a second model that re-orders the retrieved chunks by relevance.","Postgres can do vector search through the `pgvector` extension, so you don''t always need a separate database."],"deeper":"### Where RAG systems break\n- **Retrieval misses:** the answer is in a table, a PDF image or split across two chunks.\n- **Query mismatch:** users ask \"can I WFH Fridays?\" and the policy says \"remote work\". Query rewriting or hybrid search helps.\n- **Stale or conflicting sources:** two versions of a policy both get retrieved.\n- **Prompt injection:** a retrieved document contains instructions. Treat retrieved text as data.\n\n### A minimal pipeline\n```text\ndocs → chunk (≈300–800 tokens, with overlap) → embed → store\nquestion → embed → top-k search (+ keyword) → re-rank → prompt → answer + citations\n```\n\n### What to measure\n1. **Retrieval:** is the right chunk in the top k? (recall@k)\n2. **Faithfulness:** does the answer stick to the retrieved context?\n3. **Answer quality:** is it correct and useful for the question?","sources":[{"title":"Lewis et al., Retrieval-Augmented Generation for Knowledge-Intensive NLP Tasks (2020)","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.11401"},{"title":"pgvector on GitHub","url":"https://github.com/pgvector/pgvector"},{"title":"Anthropic: Contextual retrieval","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/news/contextual-retrieval"}],"check":{"prompt":"Your RAG bot gives wrong answers about a policy. The policy text is in your documents. What should you check first?","options":["Switch to a bigger LLM","Whether the right chunk is actually being retrieved for that question","Raise the temperature","Fine-tune the model on the policy"],"answer":1,"explanation":"If the correct passage isn''t in the retrieved context, no model can answer from it. Inspect what retrieval returns before changing anything else."},"interview":[{"id":"rag-iq1","q":"What is RAG and why is it used?","level":"beginner","pro":false,"answer":"1. Retrieval-Augmented Generation: retrieve relevant documents, add them to the prompt, then generate.\n2. Solves knowledge cutoff and lack of private data without retraining.\n3. Knowledge is easy to update: change the documents, not the model.\n4. Enables citations, which makes answers verifiable.","short":"RAG is retrieve, then generate. When a question comes in, we search our documents for the relevant parts and put them in the prompt, so the model answers from our data with citations. It''s how you get an LLM to know your company''s information without retraining it.","detail":"Good candidates mention that RAG is mostly a search problem. Talk about chunking, embeddings, hybrid search and evaluating retrieval. That signals you''ve thought about it beyond the diagram.","followups":[{"q":"What''s an embedding?","a":"A list of numbers representing the meaning of text, so similar meanings end up close together and can be found with similarity search."},{"q":"How do you pick chunk size?","a":"Experiment against real questions. Start around a few hundred tokens with some overlap and measure retrieval quality."}],"mistakes":["Describing RAG as training the model on your documents","Ignoring retrieval quality and evaluation"]},{"id":"rag-iq2","q":"How would you evaluate a RAG system?","level":"intermediate","pro":false,"answer":"1. Build a test set of real questions with the correct source documents and answers.\n2. Measure retrieval: is the correct chunk in the top k?\n3. Measure faithfulness: does the answer only use the retrieved context?\n4. Measure answer quality, using human review or a carefully checked LLM grader.\n5. Re-run the set whenever you change chunking, models or prompts.","short":"I''d split it into two parts: retrieval and generation. First, does search find the right chunk for each test question? Then, given the right context, is the answer correct and faithful to it? Keep a fixed test set and re-run it after every change.","detail":"Separating retrieval from generation is the key insight: it tells you which half to fix. Mention monitoring in production too, like tracking questions where users click \"not helpful\".","followups":[{"q":"Can you use an LLM to grade answers?","a":"Yes, as a scalable check, but validate it against human judgments first, because graders have their own biases."}],"mistakes":["Evaluating only by trying a few questions manually"]},{"id":"rag-iq3","q":"Design a RAG system for 50,000 internal documents with permissions.","level":"advanced","pro":true,"answer":"1. Ingestion: parse documents, chunk them, embed them, and store chunks with metadata (source, date, access groups).\n2. Retrieval: hybrid search filtered by the user''s permissions before ranking.\n3. Re-rank the top results, then build the prompt with citations.\n4. Keep the index fresh with incremental re-indexing on document changes.\n5. Evaluation set, logging and feedback loop.","short":"I''d store each chunk with its source and access groups, and filter by the user''s permissions at query time, before ranking, so nobody can retrieve what they can''t read. Then hybrid search, a re-ranker, and a prompt that answers only from the context with citations. Ingestion runs incrementally as documents change, and I''d track quality with a fixed evaluation set.","detail":"Permission filtering at retrieval time is the point most candidates miss: filtering after generation is too late, because the content has already been in the prompt. Also mention handling deletions, so removed documents stop showing up.","followups":[{"q":"Why not filter permissions after retrieval?","a":"If you filter after the LLM sees the chunk, private content can leak into the answer."}],"mistakes":["Forgetting access control entirely"]}]}'::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug) do update set category = excluded.category, title = excluded.title, data = excluded.data, updated_at = now();
insert into public.topics (slug, category, title, data) values ('react', 'development', 'React', '{"slug":"react","title":"React","category":"development","aliases":["ReactJS","React.js","JSX","hooks","virtual DOM","components"],"tagline":"A JavaScript library for building user interfaces out of small, reusable components.","level":"beginner","minutes":5,"concepts":["react-useeffect","js-event-loop","typescript-structural-typing"],"related":["javascript","typescript","rest-apis"],"popular":true,"what":"React is a JavaScript library, made at Meta and open-sourced in 2013, for building user interfaces. You describe what the screen should look like for a given piece of data, and React works out how to update the page when that data changes.","why":"Before React, most interactive pages were built by manually poking the DOM: find this element, change its text, hide that one, add a class. That works for a button. It falls apart for a feed, a chat window or a dashboard, where dozens of things depend on the same data and it''s easy to forget one. React flips it around: you stop writing *how* to change the page and start writing *what* the page should be.","how":"You build the UI out of **components**: functions that take inputs (**props**) and return markup, usually written in **JSX**. A component can hold its own **state**. When state changes, React calls the component again to get the new markup, compares it with the previous version, and applies only the differences to the real DOM. That comparison step is called **reconciliation**.","where":["Web apps at Meta (Facebook, Instagram web), Netflix, Airbnb, Atlassian and many more","React Native, which uses the same model to build iOS and Android apps","Frameworks built on top of it: Next.js, Remix, Gatsby","Internal dashboards and admin tools, where component libraries save a lot of time"],"know":["**Components** are the unit of everything. A page is a tree of components.","**Props** flow down from parent to child and are read-only. **State** is owned by a component and changes over time.","**UI is a function of state.** Change the state, and React re-renders. Don''t edit the DOM yourself.","**Hooks** (`useState`, `useEffect`, `useMemo`…) let function components hold state and run side effects. They must be called in the same order on every render.","React is a **library, not a full framework**. Routing, data fetching and builds usually come from other tools or from a framework like Next.js."],"analogy":"Think of React like a set designer working from a script. You hand over the script (\"in this scene, the cart has 3 items\"). The designer looks at the current stage, works out what''s different and moves only the props that need moving. You never tell them which chair to carry.","talk10":"React is a JavaScript library for building UIs out of components. You describe what the screen should look like for the current data, and React efficiently updates the page when that data changes.","talk30":"React lets you build an interface as a tree of components, small functions that take props and return what should be on screen. Each component can hold state, and when state changes React re-runs the component, compares the new output with the old one and updates only the parts of the DOM that actually changed. The big idea is that UI becomes a function of state, so you stop manually syncing the page with your data. It''s a library rather than a full framework, which is why people often pair it with something like Next.js for routing and server rendering.","comebacks":[{"q":"Isn''t React a framework?","a":"It''s usually called a library. It handles the view layer, and you pick or get routing, data fetching and builds from elsewhere, often from a framework like Next.js."},{"q":"Is the virtual DOM what makes React fast?","a":"Partly. It''s more that it lets React batch and minimise real DOM updates. The real performance win is that you don''t write that diffing logic by hand."},{"q":"React vs Angular?","a":"Angular is a full framework with opinions on everything. React is smaller and leaves more choices to you. Both are fine; teams pick based on how much structure they want out of the box."}],"facts":["React was first deployed on Facebook''s News Feed in 2011 and open-sourced in May 2013.","JSX isn''t required. It compiles to plain function calls, which is why it looks like HTML but behaves like JavaScript.","Hooks arrived in React 16.8 (2019) and made class components mostly optional."],"deeper":"### Reconciliation in one paragraph\nWhen state changes, React builds a new tree of elements and compares it to the previous one. If an element''s type changes (a `div` becomes a `section`), React throws away that subtree and rebuilds it. If the type is the same, it updates the changed attributes and recurses. For lists, the `key` prop tells React which item is which, so it can move items instead of recreating them. That''s why using an array index as a key causes bugs when items get reordered.\n\n### Rendering is not painting\n\"Re-render\" means React called your component function again. It does not mean the browser repainted the screen. If the output is identical, nothing touches the DOM. Unnecessary re-renders cost CPU, which is when `memo`, `useMemo` and `useCallback` help. Most apps don''t need them everywhere.\n\n### A component, end to end\n```jsx\nfunction Counter() {\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0)\n  return <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Clicked {count} times</button>\n}\n```\nClicking calls `setCount`, React schedules a re-render, `Counter` runs again with the new `count`, and only the button text changes in the DOM.\n\n### Trade-offs\n- **Flexibility vs decisions:** you choose your own router, state library and styling, which is freeing and also means more decisions.\n- **Client-side cost:** a large React bundle can slow first load. Server rendering and React Server Components exist largely to address this.\n- **Effects are easy to misuse:** many bugs come from using `useEffect` for things that could be derived during render.","sources":[{"title":"React docs: Thinking in React","url":"https://react.dev/learn/thinking-in-react"},{"title":"React docs: Preserving and resetting state","url":"https://react.dev/learn/preserving-and-resetting-state"},{"title":"React docs: You might not need an effect","url":"https://react.dev/learn/you-might-not-need-an-effect"}],"check":{"prompt":"A list renders items with `key={index}`. You insert a new item at the top. What''s the most likely problem?","options":["React throws an error about duplicate keys","State inside list items (like an input''s text) shifts to the wrong row","The list doesn''t re-render at all","Nothing, index keys are the recommended default"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Keys tell React which item is which between renders. With index keys, the old item 0 and the new item 0 look like the same component, so per-item state stays at that position while the data moves. Use a stable id from your data instead."},"interview":[{"id":"react-iq1","q":"What is React and why would you use it?","level":"beginner","pro":false,"answer":"1. A JavaScript library for building user interfaces, maintained by Meta.\n2. You build the UI from reusable components that take props and hold state.\n3. It''s declarative: you describe the UI for a given state, and React handles the DOM updates.\n4. Benefits: predictable UI, reusable components, a huge ecosystem, and React Native for mobile.","short":"React is a library for building UIs out of components. Instead of manually updating the page, I describe what it should look like for the current state and React updates the DOM for me. That makes complex interfaces much easier to reason about.","detail":"Interviewers usually want three things here: that you know it''s a *library* focused on the view layer, that you understand the *component model*, and that you can explain *declarative* UI. A good concrete example helps: \"In a todo app I don''t write code to add an `li` to the list. I add the item to state and React renders the new list.\"","followups":[{"q":"Library or framework?","a":"A library for the view layer. Frameworks like Next.js add routing, data fetching and server rendering on top."},{"q":"What is JSX?","a":"A syntax extension that compiles to `React.createElement` (or the newer JSX runtime) calls. It looks like HTML but it''s JavaScript."}],"mistakes":["Saying React is a framework without qualification","Listing features without explaining the declarative idea","Claiming the virtual DOM is the only reason it''s fast"]},{"id":"react-iq2","q":"What is the virtual DOM and what is reconciliation?","level":"intermediate","pro":false,"answer":"1. The virtual DOM is a lightweight JavaScript description of what the UI should look like.\n2. On each render, React produces a new description and compares it with the previous one.\n3. That comparison is reconciliation. It applies only the necessary changes to the real DOM.\n4. Keys help React match list items between renders.","short":"The virtual DOM is just React''s in-memory description of the UI. When state changes, React builds a new one, diffs it against the old one, and only patches what changed in the real DOM. That diffing process is called reconciliation.","detail":"React''s diff isn''t a perfect tree comparison, which would be too slow. It uses two heuristics: elements of different types produce different trees, so React rebuilds that subtree; and for lists, the `key` prop identifies items across renders. Mention that the virtual DOM is not magic speed. It''s a way to keep a declarative model while keeping DOM work small.","followups":[{"q":"Why are keys important?","a":"They let React match items across renders. Without stable keys, it may reuse the wrong component and its state."},{"q":"Is the virtual DOM faster than the real DOM?","a":"Not inherently. It avoids unnecessary real DOM work, which is the expensive part."}],"mistakes":["Saying the virtual DOM is a copy of the real DOM that renders to the screen","Forgetting to mention keys","Using \"re-render\" and \"repaint\" as if they mean the same thing"]},{"id":"react-iq3","q":"What''s the difference between props and state?","level":"beginner","pro":false,"answer":"1. Props are inputs passed from a parent. The child treats them as read-only.\n2. State is data a component owns and can change over time.\n3. Changing either one causes a re-render.\n4. If two components need the same data, lift the state up to their common parent and pass it down as props.","short":"Props are what a parent gives a component, like function arguments, and the component shouldn''t change them. State is the component''s own memory that it can update. When either changes, the component re-renders.","detail":"A good answer includes \"lifting state up\": if a filter box and a list both care about the search text, the text lives in their parent as state and is passed to both as props. That shows you understand data flowing one way, which is central to React.","followups":[{"q":"Can a child change its props?","a":"Not directly. The parent can pass a callback, and the child calls it to ask the parent to change its state."},{"q":"When would you use context?","a":"When many components at different depths need the same data, like a theme or the current user, and passing props through every level becomes noisy."}],"mistakes":["Mutating props or state directly instead of using the setter","Putting everything in global state by default"]},{"id":"react-iq4","q":"How does useEffect work, and when should you not use it?","level":"intermediate","pro":true,"answer":"1. `useEffect` runs a side effect after React commits the render to the screen.\n2. The dependency array controls when it re-runs: no array means every render, `[]` means once after mount, `[a, b]` means when a or b changes.\n3. The returned function is cleanup. It runs before the effect re-runs and on unmount.\n4. Don''t use it to derive data from props or state. Compute that during render.","short":"useEffect is for syncing a component with something outside React, like a subscription, a timer or a network request. It runs after render, re-runs when its dependencies change, and can return a cleanup function. If I''m just calculating a value from state, I don''t need an effect at all.","detail":"In development with Strict Mode, React mounts, unmounts and mounts again, so effects run twice. That surfaces missing cleanup. Common bugs are stale closures from missing dependencies, and race conditions when a slow fetch resolves after a newer one. The fix for the race is a cleanup flag or an `AbortController`.","followups":[{"q":"Why does my effect run twice in development?","a":"Strict Mode remounts components to check that effects clean up properly. It doesn''t happen in production."},{"q":"How do you avoid fetch race conditions?","a":"Set an `ignore` flag or abort the request in the cleanup function, so a stale response doesn''t overwrite newer data."}],"mistakes":["Using an effect plus state to compute derived values","Leaving out dependencies to \"stop it re-running\"","Forgetting cleanup for intervals and subscriptions"]},{"id":"react-iq5","q":"What happens when a React component re-renders?","level":"advanced","pro":true,"answer":"1. A re-render is triggered by a state change, a parent re-rendering or a context value changing.\n2. React calls the component function again and gets new elements.\n3. It reconciles them against the previous output (the render phase).\n4. It applies DOM changes and then runs effects (the commit phase).\n5. By default, children re-render when their parent does, unless memoised.","short":"When state changes, React calls the component again, which re-renders its children too by default. It compares the new output with the old, then commits only the real DOM changes and runs effects. A re-render doesn''t necessarily mean the DOM changed.","detail":"Split it into the render phase (pure, can be paused or restarted in concurrent rendering) and the commit phase (DOM mutations, layout effects, then effects). Mention that `React.memo` skips a child''s re-render if its props are shallow-equal, and that `useMemo` and `useCallback` help keep those props stable. Mention batching: several state updates in one event produce one re-render.","followups":[{"q":"Does setState update immediately?","a":"No. It schedules a re-render, and the new value is visible on the next render."},{"q":"How do you find unnecessary re-renders?","a":"The React DevTools Profiler shows what rendered and why."}],"mistakes":["Thinking a re-render always touches the DOM","Wrapping everything in memo without measuring"]}]}'::jsonb)
